As for base,

285
00:09:29,420 --> 00:09:30,460
it is actually used to hold

286
00:09:30,460 --> 00:09:31,440
what it says on it is

287
00:09:31,440 --> 00:09:32,300
called

288
00:09:32,300 --> 00:09:35,940
the base value of the trap entry function

289
00:09:35,940 --> 00:09:37,900
What does this mean?

290
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:38,720
Well, you can imagine

291
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:40,100
that when an exception occurs,

292
00:09:40,100 --> 00:09:41,540
or an interrupt,

293
00:09:41,540 --> 00:09:43,980
its main purpose is

294
00:09:43,980 --> 00:09:46,120
to make the whole machine,

295
00:09:46,120 --> 00:09:47,720
the entire execution flow of the machine,

296
00:09:47,720 --> 00:09:50,380
jump to a special handler function,

297
00:09:50,380 --> 00:09:53,220
to execute this special trap handler function.

298
00:09:53,220 --> 00:09:54,820
Now, where this trap handler function

299
00:09:54,820 --> 00:09:55,960
is placed,

300
00:09:55,960 --> 00:09:58,720
this location can actually be customized yourself.

301
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:00,400
So how do we tell the

302
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:01,460
CPU about our

303
00:10:01,460 --> 00:10:03,800
base address of this function? Where is it?

304
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:05,960
In fact, we just need to fill it in here.

305
00:10:05,960 --> 00:10:07,040
Then,

306
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:07,440
it says

307
00:10:07,440 --> 00:10:10,020
it also says we must ensure 4-byte alignment.

308
00:10:10,020 --> 00:10:10,640
That's mainly because

309
00:10:10,640 --> 00:10:11,980
as you can see,

310
00:10:11,980 --> 00:10:14,140
this register,

311
00:10:14,140 --> 00:10:14,860
it actually

312
00:10:14,860 --> 00:10:17,260
in total it's

313
00:10:17,260 --> 00:10:18,920
let's say if ours is 32-bit,

314
00:10:18,920 --> 00:10:22,380
then in total it only has 30 bits of length,

315
00:10:22,380 --> 00:10:24,800
because the last two bits are used for other content,

316
00:10:24,800 --> 00:10:25,420
right?

317
00:10:25,420 --> 00:10:27,140
So what that actually means is,

318
00:10:27,140 --> 00:10:28,540
since it's

319
00:10:28,540 --> 00:10:30,500
in other words,

320
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:31,900
if a complete address

321
00:10:31,900 --> 00:10:32,860
It should be 32 bits

322
00:10:32,860 --> 00:10:33,880
on a 32-bit machine,

323
00:10:33,880 --> 00:10:34,700
its full address

324
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:36,640
length should be 32 bits.

325
00:10:36,640 --> 00:10:38,100
So if the last two bits

326
00:10:38,100 --> 00:10:39,180
are not used,

327
00:10:39,180 --> 00:10:40,160
or are used to hold something else,

328
00:10:40,160 --> 00:10:40,880
then by default,

329
00:10:40,880 --> 00:10:41,720
the last two bits

330
00:10:41,720 --> 00:10:42,420
are both 0.

331
00:10:42,420 --> 00:10:43,820
So by default, if the last two bits

332
00:10:43,820 --> 00:10:44,380
are 0,

333
00:10:44,380 --> 00:10:46,000
then according to binary representation,

334
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,280
if the lowest two bits are 0,

335
00:10:48,280 --> 00:10:49,800
that means the whole address

336
00:10:49,800 --> 00:10:52,540
must be a multiple of 4.

337
00:10:52,540 --> 00:10:55,900
In other words, if what is stored in front is 5,

338
00:10:55,900 --> 00:10:57,000
then its actual address

339
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,060
is 20,

340
00:11:00,060 --> 00:11:02,180
because the way it is expressed here

341
00:11:02,180 --> 00:11:04,500
So he expressed it this way.

342
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:05,760
What's stored here is

343
00:11:05,760 --> 00:11:08,580
the upper 30 bits of a 32-bit number,

344
00:11:08,580 --> 00:11:09,600
the high 30 bits,

345
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:11,360
and the last two bits are actually 0 by default,

346
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:15,520
so he used them to hold other content.

347
00:11:15,520 --> 00:11:17,840
Pay attention to this.

348
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:21,640
This way we can, so to speak,

349
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:23,580
express an address using just 30 bits,

350
00:11:23,580 --> 00:11:24,580
and the last two bits,

351
00:11:24,580 --> 00:11:27,020
being left empty, can hold other content.

352
00:11:27,020 --> 00:11:30,460
That's where this four-byte alignment comes from.

353
00:11:30,460 --> 00:11:31,600
And then,

354
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:34,840
the two bits after that,

355
00:11:34,840 --> 00:11:35,680
they're called mode.

356
00:11:35,680 --> 00:11:36,460
As for mode,

357
00:11:36,460 --> 00:11:39,500
it's defined as follows:

358
00:11:39,500 --> 00:11:42,820
it can be used to control how the entry function's address is configured,

359
00:11:42,820 --> 00:11:45,860
that is, the address of this entry function,

360
00:11:45,860 --> 00:11:47,540
the way it's stored.

361
00:11:47,540 --> 00:11:48,560
It has two modes.

362
00:11:48,560 --> 00:11:49,420
One of them

363
00:11:49,420 --> 00:11:51,540
uses what's called direct mode,

364
00:11:51,540 --> 00:11:53,020
and the other is called vectored mode.

365
00:11:53,020 --> 00:11:53,660
Now, as for the values,

366
00:11:53,660 --> 00:11:54,540
they're also on this table.

367
00:11:54,540 --> 00:11:55,760
If it's direct mode,

368
00:11:55,760 --> 00:11:57,020
then the last two bits are 0.

369
00:11:57,020 --> 00:11:59,700
If it's vectored mode,

370
00:11:59,700 --> 00:12:02,140
then that means it's just 1.

371
00:12:02,140 --> 00:12:03,320
And 1 means this side here

372
00:12:03,320 --> 00:12:04,740
holds 01 in binary,

373
00:12:04,740 --> 00:12:05,180
right?

374
00:12:05,180 --> 00:12:08,740
So specifically, what is direct mode,

375
00:12:08,740 --> 00:12:09,700
and what is vectored mode?

376
00:12:09,700 --> 00:12:11,760
Let me explain that to you.

377
00:12:11,760 --> 00:12:13,880
So, direct mode

378
00:12:13,880 --> 00:12:15,320
is described in the text like this:

379
00:12:15,320 --> 00:12:18,200
after all exceptions and interrupts occur,

380
00:12:18,200 --> 00:12:21,660
the PC jumps to the address specified by base.

381
00:12:21,660 --> 00:12:23,600
So let's draw a diagram to illustrate it.

382
00:12:23,600 --> 00:12:24,660
It's actually in this form.

383
00:12:24,660 --> 00:12:26,160
That is, you can imagine

384
00:12:26,160 --> 00:12:27,040
in memory,

385
00:12:27,040 --> 00:12:29,720
we take this exception handler,

386
00:12:29,720 --> 00:12:31,460
which we call the interrupt handler here,

387
00:12:31,460 --> 00:12:32,720
strictly speaking,

388
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:34,340
it should be a trap handler.

389
00:12:34,340 --> 00:12:36,320
So this trap handler,

390
00:12:36,320 --> 00:12:38,680
this function is placed

391
00:12:38,680 --> 00:12:39,780
in the memory region starting at the ebase address.

392
00:12:39,780 --> 00:12:42,360
And then we tell this address

393
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:44,540
through this register to our CPU,

394
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:47,840
and at the same time fill in this mode value as 0.

395
00:12:47,840 --> 00:12:50,480
Then when a trap occurs on the CPU,

396
00:12:50,480 --> 00:12:54,220
it will jump to the location at the base address,

397
00:12:54,220 --> 00:12:57,280
and start executing this trap handler.

398
00:12:57,280 --> 00:13:00,300
So as you know,

399
00:13:00,300 --> 00:13:03,000
this base direct method,

400
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,320
this base direct method

401
00:13:05,320 --> 00:13:07,280
It means this handler function is only one.

402
00:13:07,280 --> 00:13:09,720
We just need to give it an address, and that's it.

403
00:13:09,720 --> 00:13:11,260
So as we all know,

404
00:13:11,260 --> 00:13:13,060
there are many kinds of exceptions and interrupts.

405
00:13:13,060 --> 00:13:14,780
For different exception handlers,

406
00:13:14,780 --> 00:13:16,520
we might need to provide different handler functions.

407
00:13:16,520 --> 00:13:20,160
So how can we, within a single handler function,

408
00:13:20,160 --> 00:13:23,960
handle multiple exceptions and interrupts?

409
00:13:23,960 --> 00:13:25,380
Well, you can imagine,

410
00:13:25,380 --> 00:13:27,300
that inside this interrupt handler function,

411
00:13:27,300 --> 00:13:28,580
if we were to write it in C,

412
00:13:28,580 --> 00:13:29,860
then there must be a

413
00:13:29,860 --> 00:13:31,880
switch-case type of logic in here,

414
00:13:31,880 --> 00:13:32,320
right?

415
00:13:32,320 --> 00:13:33,680
And then, based on

416
00:13:33,680 --> 00:13:35,820
the different exceptions and interrupts,

417
00:13:35,820 --> 00:13:37,680
which we'll talk about later,

418
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:39,980
the CPU will use other means

419
00:13:39,980 --> 00:13:40,780
to tell us,

420
00:13:40,780 --> 00:13:42,380
when this interrupt occurs.

421
00:13:42,380 --> 00:13:43,860
When this trap occurs,

422
00:13:43,860 --> 00:13:45,800
what interrupt it is,

423
00:13:45,800 --> 00:13:47,360
and/or what exception it is,

424
00:13:47,360 --> 00:13:49,440
then we can go through

425
00:13:49,440 --> 00:13:50,760
this kind of switch-case to determine,

426
00:13:50,760 --> 00:13:52,360
and then in this handler function,

427
00:13:52,360 --> 00:13:53,700
take the branch.

428
00:13:53,700 --> 00:13:57,220
This is what's called the direct method.

429
00:13:57,220 --> 00:13:59,060
Now, corresponding to it,

430
00:13:59,060 --> 00:14:00,600
there's what's called the vector method.

431
00:14:00,600 --> 00:14:01,960
What do we mean by the vector method?

432
00:14:01,960 --> 00:14:04,100
Actually, it's very simple.

433
00:14:04,100 --> 00:14:05,460
That is, it doesn't provide

434
00:14:05,460 --> 00:14:06,520
just a single function;

435
00:14:06,520 --> 00:14:08,000
it provides multiple functions.

436
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,180
And at this base address,

437
00:14:10,180 --> 00:14:11,480
what's actually stored is an array.

438
00:14:11,480 --> 00:14:12,620
You can also understand it this way:

439
00:14:12,620 --> 00:14:14,080
that is, I can have in memory

440
00:14:14,080 --> 00:14:14,820
an array defined

441
00:14:14,820 --> 00:14:15,780
This array,

442
00:14:15,780 --> 00:14:16,780
each entry is 4 bytes,

443
00:14:16,780 --> 00:14:18,020
and each one actually holds an address.

444
00:14:18,020 --> 00:14:19,120
So it's a pointer array.

445
00:14:19,120 --> 00:14:21,500
We take the base address of this pointer array,

446
00:14:21,500 --> 00:14:22,600
as the base,

447
00:14:22,600 --> 00:14:24,880
and fill it into this register,

448
00:14:24,880 --> 00:14:27,200
and then tell the CPU.

449
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:29,640
When a trap happens, the CPU,

450
00:14:29,640 --> 00:14:31,920
it will jump to this base location,

451
00:14:31,920 --> 00:14:33,500
jump to this base location.

452
00:14:33,500 --> 00:14:34,980
And then, based on this,

453
00:14:34,980 --> 00:14:37,500
the specific kind of trap,

454
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:38,860
exactly what it is,

455
00:14:38,860 --> 00:14:40,740
we'll talk about this later,

456
00:14:40,740 --> 00:14:43,400
that is, for different exceptions and interrupts,

457
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:44,420
there is actually a code,

458
00:14:44,420 --> 00:14:45,440
which is really just an index value.

459
00:14:45,440 --> 00:14:46,720
Based on this index value,

460
00:14:46,720 --> 00:14:48,540
it is then used as an index into this array.

461
00:14:48,540 --> 00:14:49,840
And then based on this,

462
00:14:49,840 --> 00:14:51,480
you can find the corresponding

463
00:14:51,480 --> 00:14:54,720
entry in the vector table where this exception lives.

464
00:14:54,720 --> 00:14:55,520
So this

465
00:14:55,520 --> 00:14:56,980
is exactly the idea of a vector.

466
00:14:56,980 --> 00:14:58,260
Now, each entry in here

467
00:14:58,260 --> 00:14:59,260
is really just an address.

468
00:14:59,260 --> 00:15:01,180
Suppose I get this

469
00:15:01,180 --> 00:15:03,380
exception number,

470
00:15:03,380 --> 00:15:05,740
say it is 2,

471
00:15:05,740 --> 00:15:07,300
then based on this base address,

472
00:15:07,300 --> 00:15:08,320
it offsets by one,

473
00:15:08,320 --> 00:15:08,980
offsets by two,

474
00:15:08,980 --> 00:15:11,120
2 times 4 bytes, right?

475
00:15:11,120 --> 00:15:12,380
If it is 3,

476
00:15:12,380 --> 00:15:13,640
then it offsets by 3 times 4 bytes.

477
00:15:13,640 --> 00:15:14,780
So say it is 2,

478
00:15:14,780 --> 00:15:16,300
then for 2 it offsets by this much,

479
00:15:16,300 --> 00:15:17,420
and after offsetting this much,

480
00:15:17,420 --> 00:15:18,740
it arrives at this position.

481
00:15:18,740 --> 00:15:19,000
right?...

482
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:20,220
So after we get to this location,

483
00:15:20,220 --> 00:15:21,740
what's stored there is another address.

484
00:15:21,740 --> 00:15:22,460
And this address,

485
00:15:22,460 --> 00:15:23,500
in fact, points to a

486
00:15:23,500 --> 00:15:25,380
handler function that actually corresponds to it.

487
00:15:25,380 --> 00:15:26,640
In other words, it actually means

488
00:15:26,640 --> 00:15:28,540
it replaces the approach where, in our code,

489
00:15:28,540 --> 00:15:29,880
we write switch-case statements ourselves

490
00:15:29,880 --> 00:15:31,980
and implements it as a

491
00:15:31,980 --> 00:15:33,860
pointer table style.

492
00:15:33,860 --> 00:15:36,120
Now think about it,

493
00:15:36,120 --> 00:15:37,320
these two approaches

494
00:15:37,320 --> 00:15:38,340
definitely have

495
00:15:38,340 --> 00:15:40,160
definitely have a purpose.

496
00:15:40,160 --> 00:15:42,220
Why is one called the

497
00:15:42,220 --> 00:15:43,700
direct mode,

498
00:15:43,700 --> 00:15:44,960
and the other called the vector mode?

499
00:15:44,960 --> 00:15:46,880
Think about why.

500
00:15:46,880 --> 00:15:50,650
Well, it's actually pretty easy to see,

501
00:15:50,650 --> 00:15:52,630
That is to say, if we use the vector method,

502
00:15:52,630 --> 00:15:54,570
if we use the approach I'm using now,

503
00:15:54,570 --> 00:15:57,710
compared to the earlier version where we formally write a switch case,

504
00:15:57,710 --> 00:15:59,970
its handling efficiency is definitely a bit higher.

505
00:15:59,970 --> 00:16:02,750
So when, if in our system,

506
00:16:02,750 --> 00:16:05,450
the interrupt or exception frequency is relatively high,

507
00:16:05,450 --> 00:16:07,910
and we frequently jump into this kind of

508
00:16:07,910 --> 00:16:10,410
interrupt and exception handler to execute,

509
00:16:10,410 --> 00:16:13,090
we actually recommend that you use this vector method

510
00:16:13,090 --> 00:16:15,310
to configure its entry address.

511
00:16:15,310 --> 00:16:16,830
In normal situations,

512
00:16:16,830 --> 00:16:18,310
we just use

513
00:16:18,310 --> 00:16:20,370
the simple base method,

514
00:16:20,370 --> 00:16:21,550
and that's actually enough.

515
00:16:21,550 --> 00:16:24,030
So the vector method

516
00:16:24,030 --> 00:16:26,410
is more suited to this kind of

517
00:16:26,410 --> 00:16:27,570
interrupt handling

518
00:16:27,570 --> 00:16:28,550
where faster response is required,

519
00:16:28,550 --> 00:16:30,170
and where interrupts occur frequently,

520
00:16:30,170 --> 00:16:31,570
and when higher efficiency is needed.

521
00:16:31,570 --> 00:16:32,690
We configure it this way,

522
00:16:32,690 --> 00:16:33,870
and that's it.

523
00:16:33,870 --> 00:16:36,170
The effect is actually the same.

524
00:16:36,170 --> 00:16:36,770
Now this one,

525
00:16:36,770 --> 00:16:39,030
is a register called MTVEC,

526
00:16:39,030 --> 00:16:40,410
which stands for Machine Trap Vector

527
00:16:40,410 --> 00:16:41,810
Base Address.

528
00:16:41,810 --> 00:16:43,510
That's what this register is.

529
00:16:43,510 --> 00:16:44,670
Very simple,

530
00:16:44,670 --> 00:16:45,810
it's used to configure

531
00:16:45,810 --> 00:16:47,210
the entry address of the trap handler.

532
00:16:47,210 --> 00:16:49,430
That's the first register.

533
00:16:49,430 --> 00:16:52,630
Now the second register,

534
00:16:52,630 --> 00:16:54,770
is called MEPC.

535
00:16:54,770 --> 00:16:56,610
What it means,

536
00:16:56,610 --> 00:16:58,630
is a kind of exception program counter

537
00:16:58,630 --> 00:17:01,490
in machine mode.

538
00:17:01,490 --> 00:17:02,810
Program counter,

539
00:17:02,810 --> 00:17:04,170
we've mentioned it many times before,

540
00:17:04,170 --> 00:17:05,170
it actually represents

541
00:17:05,170 --> 00:17:06,190
The execution of this program,

542
00:17:06,190 --> 00:17:07,850
the concept of the current position

543
00:17:07,850 --> 00:17:08,170
— right?

544
00:17:08,170 --> 00:17:09,750
So it is actually also used

545
00:17:09,750 --> 00:17:13,670
to hold this kind of information

546
00:17:13,670 --> 00:17:15,090
about the program's execution position.

547
00:17:15,090 --> 00:17:17,910
So its length is,

548
00:17:17,910 --> 00:17:20,070
for example, right now we are only using 32-bit,

549
00:17:20,070 --> 00:17:21,510
so it is 32 bits long.

550
00:17:21,510 --> 00:17:23,290
So when exactly

551
00:17:23,290 --> 00:17:24,430
is this register used?

552
00:17:24,430 --> 00:17:26,070
It is described very clearly here:

553
00:17:26,070 --> 00:17:28,750
that is, when a trap occurs,

554
00:17:28,750 --> 00:17:30,730
we know

555
00:17:30,730 --> 00:17:34,820
our program will definitely jump to

556
00:17:34,820 --> 00:17:36,860
that mtvec,

557
00:17:36,860 --> 00:17:37,880
the register we just mentioned,

558
00:17:37,880 --> 00:17:39,300
and execute at the address it holds.

559
00:17:39,300 --> 00:17:41,360
Now, as for jumping to that address to execute,

560
00:17:41,360 --> 00:17:42,640
well, everyone can imagine,

561
00:17:42,640 --> 00:17:44,960
So what actually happens is that our normal PC

562
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:47,780
the PC we keep mentioning earlier

563
00:17:47,780 --> 00:17:49,980
the value inside it actually gets replaced

564
00:17:49,980 --> 00:17:53,040
So this is what the so-called jump refers to

565
00:17:53,040 --> 00:17:54,400
that's really what this sentence means

566
00:17:54,400 --> 00:17:57,900
Now, since this PC has been replaced

567
00:17:57,900 --> 00:18:01,600
we know that exceptions and interrupts

568
00:18:01,600 --> 00:18:04,360
actually express a very fundamental concept

569
00:18:04,360 --> 00:18:06,580
We may have gone a bit too fast just now

570
00:18:06,580 --> 00:18:07,300
and skipped over it in passing

571
00:18:07,300 --> 00:18:09,160
It's this figure from earlier

572
00:18:09,160 --> 00:18:10,880
this figure right here

573
00:18:10,880 --> 00:18:13,860
that is to say, whether it's an exception or an interrupt

574
00:18:13,860 --> 00:18:17,200
it actually follows this same basic idea right here

575
00:18:17,200 --> 00:18:18,860
meaning that when our normal

576
00:18:18,860 --> 00:18:23,720
these two lines actually represent our normal control flow

577
00:18:23,720 --> 00:18:28,200
and this is our trap handler

578
00:18:28,200 --> 00:18:29,260
this is the exception control flow

579
00:18:29,260 --> 00:18:32,120
So what this dotted line expresses is

580
00:18:32,120 --> 00:18:35,740
that when, say, an interrupt or an exception occurs

581
00:18:35,740 --> 00:18:40,780
our execution flow will jump from the normal control flow to

582
00:18:40,780 --> 00:18:42,860
this exception control flow

583
00:18:42,860 --> 00:18:44,540
to execute a special handler function

584
00:18:44,540 --> 00:18:46,160
but after the handling is done,

585
00:18:46,160 --> 00:18:47,780
it's actually not finished

586
00:18:47,780 --> 00:18:49,020
it still has to come back

587
00:18:49,020 --> 00:18:49,660
see what I mean?

588
00:18:49,660 --> 00:18:50,880
it still has to come back

589
00:18:50,880 --> 00:18:53,040
so this jumping out and coming back,

590
00:18:53,040 --> 00:18:54,880
this process is a bit like our

591
00:18:54,880 --> 00:18:57,720
function call execution

592
00:18:57,720 --> 00:18:58,500
but,

593
00:18:58,500 --> 00:19:00,240
as you know, this call and execution

594
00:19:00,240 --> 00:19:00,980
is not something that happens

595
00:19:00,980 --> 00:19:02,760
under our control

596
00:19:02,760 --> 00:19:06,180
it's a hardware handling behavior of the CPU

597
00:19:06,180 --> 00:19:09,080
so since it has to jump out,

598
00:19:09,080 --> 00:19:09,940
when it jumps out,

599
00:19:09,940 --> 00:19:10,860
as we just said,

600
00:19:10,860 --> 00:19:11,700
when it jumps out,

601
00:19:11,700 --> 00:19:13,820
Our PC value will actually be replaced

602
00:19:13,820 --> 00:19:15,200
Then, when we come back,

603
00:19:15,200 --> 00:19:16,200
for it to continue executing,

604
00:19:16,200 --> 00:19:18,580
this PC value must be restored

605
00:19:18,580 --> 00:19:19,400
Think about it, right?

606
00:19:19,400 --> 00:19:20,520
It must be restored

607
00:19:20,520 --> 00:19:23,540
And since it needs to be restored,

608
00:19:23,540 --> 00:19:25,100
that means at the time of the jump,

609
00:19:25,100 --> 00:19:26,940
this original PC value

610
00:19:26,940 --> 00:19:27,900
because we've already jumped to here,

611
00:19:27,900 --> 00:19:31,000
the PC value gets replaced with the value at this location

612
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:31,320
Right?

613
00:19:31,320 --> 00:19:33,400
So this PC value has to be saved

614
00:19:33,400 --> 00:19:34,660
And saved where?

615
00:19:34,660 --> 00:19:36,320
Actually, it's saved into what we just mentioned,

616
00:19:36,320 --> 00:19:37,740
this thing called EPC

617
00:19:37,740 --> 00:19:39,420
which is specifically used for exceptions,

618
00:19:39,420 --> 00:19:41,600
used to save, when an exception occurs,

619
00:19:41,600 --> 00:19:43,200
at the moment it occurs,

620
00:19:43,200 --> 00:19:45,220
the PC value of that exception

621
00:19:45,220 --> 00:19:46,680
So when it comes back,

622
00:19:46,680 --> 00:19:49,320
we restore the values that were in here,

623
00:19:49,320 --> 00:19:51,220
and then it can continue executing downward.

624
00:19:51,220 --> 00:19:52,640
There is actually a game like this,

625
00:19:52,640 --> 00:19:53,660
which maybe just now,

626
00:19:53,660 --> 00:19:55,320
when we were talking about this register,

627
00:19:55,320 --> 00:19:57,060
was mentioned briefly in passing.

628
00:19:57,060 --> 00:19:58,260
Let me go over it for you again.

629
00:19:58,260 --> 00:20:00,360
Okay.

630
00:20:00,360 --> 00:20:03,840
So let's go back to this MEPC we were just talking about.

631
00:20:03,840 --> 00:20:04,900
So now everyone knows,

632
00:20:04,900 --> 00:20:06,980
this MEPC, this register,

633
00:20:06,980 --> 00:20:09,060
is actually used to save

634
00:20:09,060 --> 00:20:10,920
when a trap occurs,

635
00:20:10,920 --> 00:20:13,660
since this PC gets replaced,

636
00:20:13,660 --> 00:20:15,180
we have to take that

637
00:20:15,180 --> 00:20:18,760
original PC value and save it.

638
00:20:18,760 --> 00:20:19,700
Where do we save it?

639
00:20:19,700 --> 00:20:22,260
It gets saved into this MEPC register.

640
00:20:22,260 --> 00:20:24,880
So what we just mentioned

641
00:20:24,880 --> 00:20:26,180
That is, exiting this CHAP.

642
00:20:26,180 --> 00:20:27,800
So after the CHAP processing is done,

643
00:20:27,800 --> 00:20:28,320
we have to come back.

644
00:20:28,320 --> 00:20:28,840
When we come back,

645
00:20:28,840 --> 00:20:30,680
how do we make that PC value

646
00:20:30,680 --> 00:20:34,120
get restored back to the MEPC value we saved?

647
00:20:34,120 --> 00:20:35,160
Well, we'll talk about that later.

648
00:20:35,160 --> 00:20:36,320
It uses a special instruction,

649
00:20:36,320 --> 00:20:38,600
called Mret here.

650
00:20:38,600 --> 00:20:40,600
Ret just means return.

651
00:20:40,600 --> 00:20:41,820
This one,

652
00:20:41,820 --> 00:20:44,220
we'll talk about it again later.

653
00:20:44,220 --> 00:20:46,300
It's not quite the same as the return instruction

654
00:20:46,300 --> 00:20:46,940
that we normally call.

655
00:20:46,940 --> 00:20:49,640
It's a special instruction

656
00:20:49,640 --> 00:20:52,420
used when returning from an exception

657
00:20:52,420 --> 00:20:53,780
back to normal execution.

658
00:20:53,780 --> 00:20:55,160
So when this instruction is called,

659
00:20:55,160 --> 00:20:57,240
it actually makes use of the MEPC

660
00:20:57,240 --> 00:20:58,580
that we just talked about.

661
00:20:58,580 --> 00:21:00,880
So it will take that original

662
00:21:00,880 --> 00:21:02,800
value and restore it into PC,

663
00:21:02,800 --> 00:21:04,200
which achieves the effect of returning.

664
00:21:04,200 --> 00:21:05,240
Combine this with that diagram from earlier

665
00:21:05,240 --> 00:21:07,680
and take a moment to understand it again.

666
00:21:07,680 --> 00:21:12,480
This is the effect of mepc,

667
00:21:12,480 --> 00:21:14,240
the effect that mepc is meant to achieve.

668
00:21:14,240 --> 00:21:16,840
Now, on this point,

669
00:21:16,840 --> 00:21:17,620
I should also mention here on my end,

670
00:21:17,620 --> 00:21:18,580
namely that

671
00:21:18,580 --> 00:21:19,720
this restoration,

672
00:21:19,720 --> 00:21:20,600
that is, at the time of saving,

673
00:21:20,600 --> 00:21:22,380
what mepc saves

674
00:21:22,380 --> 00:21:23,320
might be the current instruction,

675
00:21:23,320 --> 00:21:24,780
or it might be the address of the next instruction.

676
00:21:24,780 --> 00:21:27,020
As for when the current address gets saved,

677
00:21:27,020 --> 00:21:28,400
and when the next address gets saved,

678
00:21:28,400 --> 00:21:30,340
we will cover that with you later on.

679
00:21:30,340 --> 00:21:31,280
But in any case, to sum up,

680
00:21:31,280 --> 00:21:33,140
it is always used to save

681
00:21:33,140 --> 00:21:34,660
a value related to the PC

682
00:21:34,660 --> 00:21:36,500
Now, what happens next?

683
00:21:36,500 --> 00:21:38,040
When we return,

684
00:21:38,040 --> 00:21:39,180
the value is restored into the PC,

685
00:21:39,180 --> 00:21:40,700
achieving the effect of a return.

686
00:21:40,700 --> 00:21:45,880
So in the normal handling process,

687
00:21:45,880 --> 00:21:46,600
it's mentioned here

688
00:21:46,600 --> 00:21:47,700
that its value can also be modified

689
00:21:47,700 --> 00:21:49,900
to change its return address.

690
00:21:49,900 --> 00:21:51,820
In our later examples,

691
00:21:51,820 --> 00:21:54,600
we'll also cover this kind of usage.

692
00:21:54,600 --> 00:21:55,800
So when we get to it,

693
00:21:55,800 --> 00:21:56,620
we'll look at it then.

694
00:21:56,620 --> 00:21:57,460
For now, here,

695
00:21:57,460 --> 00:21:59,620
just knowing that this exists is enough.

696
00:21:59,620 --> 00:22:02,860
This is what we need to understand,

697
00:22:02,860 --> 00:22:04,020
that is, the second register

698
00:22:04,020 --> 00:22:07,080
related to exception handling,

699
00:22:07,080 --> 00:22:08,800
called MEPC.

700
00:22:08,800 --> 00:22:15,440
Now, the third one we need to understand

701
00:22:15,440 --> 00:22:17,020
It's called Machine Cause.

702
00:22:17,020 --> 00:22:18,680
Cause means the reason.

703
00:22:18,680 --> 00:22:20,000
Because, because, right?

704
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:20,940
It means the reason.

705
00:22:20,940 --> 00:22:22,300
Now, as we mentioned earlier,

706
00:22:22,300 --> 00:22:24,060
when a trap occurs,

707
00:22:24,060 --> 00:22:25,660
just like that one earlier,

708
00:22:25,660 --> 00:22:28,600
when we were talking about mtvec,

709
00:22:28,600 --> 00:22:29,180
we also mentioned that

710
00:22:29,180 --> 00:22:31,580
the CPU

711
00:22:31,580 --> 00:22:33,760
will record the cause of this exception

712
00:22:33,760 --> 00:22:35,140
— specifically, what kind of exception it is,

713
00:22:35,140 --> 00:22:36,820
what kind of interrupt it is —

714
00:22:36,820 --> 00:22:39,160
and tell us through this register.

715
00:22:39,160 --> 00:22:41,180
So we can read the value in this register

716
00:22:41,180 --> 00:22:42,540
and just do a switch case,

717
00:22:42,540 --> 00:22:42,840
right?

718
00:22:42,840 --> 00:22:44,180
Taking different branches.

719
00:22:44,180 --> 00:22:46,640
So this is used to tell us

720
00:22:46,640 --> 00:22:48,780
and store the exception code.

721
00:22:48,780 --> 00:22:49,780
The so-called exception code,

722
00:22:49,780 --> 00:22:53,920
that is, the register holding the cause value.

723
00:22:53,920 --> 00:22:55,460
Now, one thing mentioned here is that

724
00:22:55,460 --> 00:22:56,640
on this side they have written another one,

725
00:22:56,640 --> 00:22:58,140
the spec says that

726
00:22:58,140 --> 00:22:59,280
this field,

727
00:22:59,280 --> 00:23:01,960
its attribute is called WRL.

728
00:23:01,960 --> 00:23:03,560
Now, as for WRL,

729
00:23:03,560 --> 00:23:05,040
what they write here is

730
00:23:05,040 --> 00:23:08,500
it stands for write read only legal values.

731
00:23:08,500 --> 00:23:10,620
So what it really means is that

732
00:23:10,620 --> 00:23:14,160
when we read or write this field,

733
00:23:14,160 --> 00:23:17,320
it must ensure that its value

734
00:23:17,320 --> 00:23:18,580
for example, when you write to it,

735
00:23:18,580 --> 00:23:20,180
its value must be legal.

736
00:23:20,180 --> 00:23:22,180
If it is an illegal value,

737
00:23:22,180 --> 00:23:24,560
meaning one not defined in our spec,

738
00:23:24,560 --> 00:23:26,360
or some value it does not recognize,

739
00:23:26,360 --> 00:23:28,220
it may trigger a further exception.

740
00:23:28,220 --> 00:23:29,660
Please keep this in mind.

741
00:23:29,660 --> 00:23:33,740
This is the meaning of WRL.

742
00:23:33,740 --> 00:23:34,780
Let's talk about it briefly.

743
00:23:34,780 --> 00:23:36,060
So this

744
00:23:36,060 --> 00:23:38,680
this register,

745
00:23:38,680 --> 00:23:40,440
that is, when an exception occurs,

746
00:23:40,440 --> 00:23:41,860
this Hart, that is, the CPU,

747
00:23:41,860 --> 00:23:43,360
it will set this register,

748
00:23:43,360 --> 00:23:44,540
to tell us about this trap,

749
00:23:44,540 --> 00:23:45,840
the specific reason it happened.

750
00:23:45,840 --> 00:23:47,400
The specific reason is actually a value,

751
00:23:47,400 --> 00:23:49,000
this value.

752
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:51,480
And then when it encodes it,

753
00:23:51,480 --> 00:23:52,340
that is, the whole thing,

754
00:23:52,340 --> 00:23:53,280
for example, if it's 32 bits,

755
00:23:53,280 --> 00:23:54,020
its most significant bit,

756
00:23:54,020 --> 00:23:56,120
the most significant bit is used to tell us

757
00:23:56,120 --> 00:23:58,400
whether it is an exception

758
00:23:58,400 --> 00:24:00,260
or an interrupt,

759
00:24:00,260 --> 00:24:01,180
because as we mentioned earlier,

760
00:24:01,180 --> 00:24:02,200
traps fall into two categories.

761
00:24:02,200 --> 00:24:03,780
It's either an interrupt or an exception.

762
00:24:03,780 --> 00:24:06,060
So here,

763
00:24:06,060 --> 00:24:07,400
if this highest bit

764
00:24:07,400 --> 00:24:10,060
that is, the highest bit of this 32-bit field

765
00:24:10,060 --> 00:24:12,000
is 1,

766
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:15,460
it means the trap that just occurred is an interrupt.

767
00:24:15,460 --> 00:24:16,720
Otherwise,

768
00:24:16,720 --> 00:24:17,900
if it is 0,

769
00:24:17,900 --> 00:24:18,680
otherwise it's 0,

770
00:24:18,680 --> 00:24:20,060
and 0 means it's an exception.

771
00:24:20,060 --> 00:24:25,700
So by looking at this highest field, we can quickly tell apart

772
00:24:25,700 --> 00:24:27,540
whether what just happened is an interrupt or an exception.

773
00:24:27,540 --> 00:24:31,060
Now, what about the remaining part?

774
00:24:31,060 --> 00:24:33,280
As we just said, it's the specific

775
00:24:33,280 --> 00:24:35,620
which interrupt it actually is,

776
00:24:35,620 --> 00:24:37,340
or which exception it actually is,

777
00:24:37,340 --> 00:24:38,160
the specific value.

778
00:24:38,160 --> 00:24:39,000
For this value,

779
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:41,280
the specification also has a table

780
00:24:41,280 --> 00:24:42,200
that defines

781
00:24:42,200 --> 00:24:42,980
You can take a look

782
00:24:42,980 --> 00:24:46,100
at this table in the privileged spec, section 3.6,

783
00:24:46,100 --> 00:24:47,280
Table 3.6.

784
00:24:47,280 --> 00:24:49,580
Now, as you can see from this table,

785
00:24:49,580 --> 00:24:50,740
what it actually means is that

786
00:24:50,740 --> 00:24:51,920
this highest bit here,

787
00:24:51,920 --> 00:24:53,360
meaning that this side is all ones,

788
00:24:53,360 --> 00:24:53,640
right?

789
00:24:53,640 --> 00:24:54,520
If it's all ones,

790
00:24:54,520 --> 00:24:55,440
then that means this side is

791
00:24:55,440 --> 00:24:56,440
this category is interrupts.

792
00:24:56,440 --> 00:24:58,520
And if the highest bit of this category is 0,

793
00:24:58,520 --> 00:24:59,600
then they are all exceptions.

794
00:24:59,600 --> 00:25:01,160
And it also defines,

795
00:25:01,160 --> 00:25:04,040
for instance, these 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 respectively.

796
00:25:04,040 --> 00:25:06,240
When the front part is an interrupt,

797
00:25:06,240 --> 00:25:08,400
what do 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 each mean?

798
00:25:08,400 --> 00:25:11,260
And when it's an exception,

799
00:25:11,260 --> 00:25:14,940
what exactly do the exception values 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 mean?

800
00:25:14,940 --> 00:25:19,220
Now, for us,

801
00:25:19,220 --> 00:25:20,920
Today we're mainly going to talk about exceptions.

802
00:25:20,920 --> 00:25:24,740
So for interrupts,

803
00:25:24,740 --> 00:25:26,400
we'll leave them aside for now.

804
00:25:26,400 --> 00:25:28,560
As we can see, exceptions are divided into many kinds.

805
00:25:28,560 --> 00:25:31,280
For example, instruction address misaligned,

806
00:25:31,280 --> 00:25:33,380
instruction access fault,

807
00:25:33,380 --> 00:25:36,220
that is, the access failed,

808
00:25:36,220 --> 00:25:38,240
and illegal instruction, and so on.

809
00:25:38,240 --> 00:25:41,080
In other words, when the CPU detects that our program

810
00:25:41,080 --> 00:25:43,980
has triggered one of these problems,

811
00:25:43,980 --> 00:25:45,780
it will raise an exception,

812
00:25:45,780 --> 00:25:46,700
and notify us.

813
00:25:46,700 --> 00:25:50,340
It then jumps to the specific exception handler

814
00:25:50,340 --> 00:25:51,760
logic to execute.

815
00:25:51,760 --> 00:25:53,040
That's roughly what it means.

816
00:25:53,040 --> 00:25:55,660
This is the third register,

817
00:25:55,660 --> 00:25:57,380
which is mcause.

818
00:25:57,380 --> 00:26:01,840
Then there is also one called mtval.

819
00:26:01,840 --> 00:26:03,520
What this actually means is that

820
00:26:03,520 --> 00:26:05,100
it assists that mcause register.

821
00:26:05,100 --> 00:26:07,160
Because mcause is just a single value that tells us specifically about this exception—

822
00:26:07,160 --> 00:26:09,820
for instance, what kind of exception it is.

823
00:26:09,820 --> 00:26:11,620
For example, what kind of exception it is.

824
00:26:11,620 --> 00:26:14,880
Now, when certain exceptions occur,

825
00:26:14,880 --> 00:26:17,900
it may also need to give us more detailed information.

826
00:26:17,900 --> 00:26:20,040
So that single register from earlier might not be enough.

827
00:26:20,040 --> 00:26:21,920
So this mtval register,

828
00:26:21,920 --> 00:26:23,160
together with mcause,

829
00:26:23,160 --> 00:26:26,160
acts as a supplementary use of mcause—

830
00:26:26,160 --> 00:26:26,940
an additional description.

831
00:26:26,940 --> 00:26:28,800
Through it, the CPU can

832
00:26:28,800 --> 00:26:31,800
that is, the CPU can use this register,

833
00:26:31,800 --> 00:26:33,520
along with the mcause from before,

834
00:26:33,520 --> 00:26:35,700
to give us more detailed

835
00:26:35,700 --> 00:26:37,400
information about the exception.

836
00:26:37,400 --> 00:26:41,000
Just get a rough idea of this,

837
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:42,060
and I won't say more about it.

838
00:26:42,060 --> 00:26:46,240
Now there is another register,

839
00:26:46,240 --> 00:26:47,340
which is also quite important,

840
00:26:47,340 --> 00:26:48,520
called mstatus.

841
00:26:48,520 --> 00:26:53,720
It is used to describe some status information.

842
00:26:53,720 --> 00:26:58,960
It describes some status information.

843
00:26:58,960 --> 00:27:00,820
So let's look at it,

844
00:27:00,820 --> 00:27:04,650
let's take a look.

845
00:27:04,650 --> 00:27:06,330
That is to say, the whole thing is 32 bits,

846
00:27:06,330 --> 00:27:08,790
and it is divided very finely.

847
00:27:08,790 --> 00:27:10,630
Every bit has a specific meaning.

848
00:27:10,630 --> 00:27:11,510
Among them,

849
00:27:11,510 --> 00:27:13,010
here another special one appears.

850
00:27:13,010 --> 00:27:16,950
This term is called WPRI.

851
00:27:16,950 --> 00:27:18,150
So what is written here is,

852
00:27:18,150 --> 00:27:20,030
WPRI means,

853
00:27:20,030 --> 00:27:21,190
Write Positive,

854
00:27:21,190 --> 00:27:23,570
that is, on writes it keeps the reserved value.

855
00:27:23,570 --> 00:27:24,970
When reading, it is ignored.

856
00:27:24,970 --> 00:27:25,750
So in fact, that is to say,

857
00:27:25,750 --> 00:27:26,370
for this part,

858
00:27:26,370 --> 00:27:27,910
it actually does not,

859
00:27:27,910 --> 00:27:29,510
the specification does not actually use it.

860
00:27:29,510 --> 00:27:30,270
That is to say,

861
00:27:30,270 --> 00:27:32,410
when it comes to writing

862
00:27:32,410 --> 00:27:36,010
for us programmers, actually,

863
00:27:36,010 --> 00:27:36,570
what it really means is

864
00:27:36,570 --> 00:27:39,250
when we operate on this timer,

865
00:27:39,250 --> 00:27:40,730
then this part,

866
00:27:40,730 --> 00:27:42,570
as much as possible,

867
00:27:42,570 --> 00:27:44,350
don't write to it

868
00:27:44,350 --> 00:27:45,230
and if there is a write operation,

869
00:27:45,230 --> 00:27:46,770
try not to touch it

870
00:27:46,770 --> 00:27:47,510
whatever value was there originally,

871
00:27:47,510 --> 00:27:48,830
preserve it

872
00:27:48,830 --> 00:27:49,630
so the real idea is this:

873
00:27:49,630 --> 00:27:51,010
when we write to this timer,

874
00:27:51,010 --> 00:27:52,750
first read out the timer value,

875
00:27:52,750 --> 00:27:54,870
then modify just the bits you need,

876
00:27:54,870 --> 00:27:57,190
and then write it back, and that's it

877
00:27:57,190 --> 00:27:58,130
so this

878
00:27:58,130 --> 00:28:02,390
means when you write, you must ensure the values inside stay unchanged,

879
00:28:02,390 --> 00:28:04,130
don't disturb the original values in there

880
00:28:04,130 --> 00:28:05,330
and when reading,

881
00:28:05,330 --> 00:28:05,990
The values read out of here

882
00:28:05,990 --> 00:28:07,410
are meaningless to you right now,

883
00:28:07,410 --> 00:28:08,690
so you can just ignore them for the moment.

884
00:28:08,690 --> 00:28:10,810
This part is actually reserved by the specification,

885
00:28:10,810 --> 00:28:12,010
so let's just leave it alone.

886
00:28:12,010 --> 00:28:13,070
As for the rest,

887
00:28:13,070 --> 00:28:14,690
the parts that are already defined,

888
00:28:14,690 --> 00:28:17,310
those are what we can read and write.

889
00:28:17,310 --> 00:28:19,510
Now, among them,

890
00:28:19,510 --> 00:28:23,590
it splits this into many fields.

891
00:28:23,590 --> 00:28:24,610
We'll just pick out

892
00:28:24,610 --> 00:28:26,510
the ones we're dealing with

893
00:28:26,510 --> 00:28:27,270
and walk through them for you.

894
00:28:27,270 --> 00:28:30,630
The parts that aren't highlighted in that color

895
00:28:30,630 --> 00:28:31,970
also have specific meanings,

896
00:28:31,970 --> 00:28:32,710
but since we're not using them,

897
00:28:32,710 --> 00:28:34,310
we won't talk about them here.

898
00:28:34,310 --> 00:28:36,930
Now this one, for instance, let's first look at the red part.

899
00:28:36,930 --> 00:28:40,050
That is, these lowest bits,

900
00:28:40,050 --> 00:28:42,330
namely bit 0, bit 1, and bit 3.

901
00:28:42,330 --> 00:28:44,230
So it actually follows a very regular pattern.

902
00:28:44,230 --> 00:28:44,830
Take a look, everyone.

903
00:28:44,830 --> 00:28:47,070
That is, if you look at the naming, it's all like this:

904
00:28:47,070 --> 00:28:50,990
You see, they all come in these groups.

905
00:28:50,990 --> 00:28:52,150
Group by group.

906
00:28:52,150 --> 00:28:54,350
For example, over here they are all IEI.

907
00:28:54,350 --> 00:28:55,590
Things like IEIE.

908
00:28:55,590 --> 00:28:57,510
And it's only the front part

909
00:28:57,510 --> 00:29:00,010
where they swap in a USM.

910
00:29:00,010 --> 00:29:01,850
Now, you can probably guess what USM stands for.

911
00:29:01,850 --> 00:29:03,290
It's actually what we've been talking about —

912
00:29:03,290 --> 00:29:05,090
these privilege modes:

913
00:29:05,090 --> 00:29:06,030
the user mode,

914
00:29:06,030 --> 00:29:07,570
the super mode, and

915
00:29:07,570 --> 00:29:09,390
the machine mode.

916
00:29:09,390 --> 00:29:10,750
They all follow this pattern.

917
00:29:10,750 --> 00:29:13,190
MSU.

918
00:29:13,190 --> 00:29:14,810
So when we discuss them here,

919
00:29:14,810 --> 00:29:16,810
we just use an X in front as a placeholder.

920
00:29:16,810 --> 00:29:18,090
Just keep that in mind.

921
00:29:18,090 --> 00:29:20,730
It's divided into xIE, xPIE, and xPP

922
00:29:20,730 --> 00:29:21,810
That's what it means

923
00:29:21,810 --> 00:29:24,990
So these specific three fields are split into three categories

924
00:29:24,990 --> 00:29:26,610
What do these three categories actually represent?

925
00:29:26,610 --> 00:29:28,090
Let's look at IE first

926
00:29:28,090 --> 00:29:29,270
As for IE,

927
00:29:29,270 --> 00:29:30,850
it's actually an English abbreviation

928
00:29:30,850 --> 00:29:33,510
which stands for interrupt enable

929
00:29:33,510 --> 00:29:35,450
that is, the interrupt enable bit

930
00:29:35,450 --> 00:29:37,570
meaning that this

931
00:29:37,570 --> 00:29:39,590
these three bits

932
00:29:39,590 --> 00:29:41,390
actually control our

933
00:29:41,390 --> 00:29:43,490
under different modes,

934
00:29:43,490 --> 00:29:44,470
whether the global interrupt

935
00:29:44,470 --> 00:29:47,490
should be enabled or disabled

936
00:29:47,490 --> 00:29:49,370
So when we want to

937
00:29:49,370 --> 00:29:50,850
for example, when we want to

938
00:29:50,850 --> 00:29:52,270
take the interrupts in

939
00:29:52,270 --> 00:29:54,250
machine mode and enable them,

940
00:29:54,250 --> 00:29:55,510
we set this bit

941
00:29:55,510 --> 00:29:57,750
That is, at this MRE field,

942
00:29:57,750 --> 00:29:58,950
just set it to 1,

943
00:29:58,950 --> 00:29:59,870
and that expresses this meaning.

944
00:29:59,870 --> 00:30:00,890
If you want to disable it,

945
00:30:00,890 --> 00:30:02,910
just set it to 0.

946
00:30:02,910 --> 00:30:06,180
At the same time,

947
00:30:06,180 --> 00:30:07,900
we can also, by reading

948
00:30:07,900 --> 00:30:09,460
one of these fields,

949
00:30:09,460 --> 00:30:10,380
know whether the current interrupt

950
00:30:10,380 --> 00:30:12,220
is enabled or disabled.

951
00:30:12,220 --> 00:30:12,800
That is to say, when writing,

952
00:30:12,800 --> 00:30:14,960
you are setting the interrupt,

953
00:30:14,960 --> 00:30:15,860
disabling or enabling it.

954
00:30:15,860 --> 00:30:16,760
And when reading,

955
00:30:16,760 --> 00:30:17,720
you can find out whether this interrupt

956
00:30:17,720 --> 00:30:19,340
is currently enabled or disabled.

957
00:30:19,340 --> 00:30:20,580
This is XRE.

958
00:30:20,580 --> 00:30:22,660
Now, what about XPRE?

959
00:30:22,660 --> 00:30:23,560
This P here,

960
00:30:23,560 --> 00:30:24,820
it is connected with this RE that follows it.

961
00:30:24,820 --> 00:30:26,260
Also the concept of interrupt enable,

962
00:30:26,260 --> 00:30:28,760
and this P actually stands for 'previous'.

963
00:30:28,760 --> 00:30:31,380
It means previous,

964
00:30:31,380 --> 00:30:37,800
as in the state of the previous interrupt enable bit.

965
00:30:37,800 --> 00:30:39,900
What it's saying here is essentially that

966
00:30:39,900 --> 00:30:44,240
when a trap occurs, it is used to save the

967
00:30:44,240 --> 00:30:46,480
xRE value from before the trap occurred.

968
00:30:46,480 --> 00:30:48,280
In other words, suppose this,

969
00:30:48,280 --> 00:30:49,880
for instance, at the very beginning,

970
00:30:49,880 --> 00:30:53,480
the interrupt is enabled.

971
00:30:53,480 --> 00:30:55,480
Say the value here was originally enabled,

972
00:30:55,480 --> 00:30:56,720
say it was 1.

973
00:30:56,720 --> 00:30:58,980
Then, when we are in machine mode and

974
00:30:58,980 --> 00:31:00,200
an interrupt

975
00:31:00,200 --> 00:31:01,980
or an exception occurs,

976
00:31:01,980 --> 00:31:04,320
it will take the value in here

977
00:31:04,320 --> 00:31:06,560
and first save it into this one,

978
00:31:06,560 --> 00:31:08,620
save it into this one.

979
00:31:08,620 --> 00:31:10,640
Then, once the exception has been handled,

980
00:31:10,640 --> 00:31:11,820
it will use the value in this one

981
00:31:11,820 --> 00:31:13,080
and then perform the corresponding restoration.

982
00:31:13,080 --> 00:31:15,680
It has this kind of use built into it.

983
00:31:15,680 --> 00:31:19,780
This is XPI.

984
00:31:19,780 --> 00:31:21,480
Then XPP,

985
00:31:21,480 --> 00:31:22,680
as for the P,

986
00:31:22,680 --> 00:31:24,600
this P actually,

987
00:31:24,600 --> 00:31:26,920
in English it should be,

988
00:31:26,920 --> 00:31:28,960
the first P is still previous,

989
00:31:28,960 --> 00:31:29,800
meaning the previous one.

990
00:31:29,800 --> 00:31:31,160
As for the second P,

991
00:31:31,160 --> 00:31:31,920
it's privilege,

992
00:31:31,920 --> 00:31:33,280
which means access rights.

993
00:31:33,280 --> 00:31:35,480
So it's previous privilege.

994
00:31:35,480 --> 00:31:37,000
Now, for this one,

995
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:37,800
it's also written right here,

996
00:31:37,800 --> 00:31:38,800
it is used to store

997
00:31:38,800 --> 00:31:42,740
the privilege level from before this trap occurred,

998
00:31:42,740 --> 00:31:44,880
the previous privilege level from before,

999
00:31:44,880 --> 00:31:46,320
this privilege value.

1000
00:31:46,320 --> 00:31:48,180
In other words,

1001
00:31:48,180 --> 00:31:49,400
So, for example,

1002
00:31:49,400 --> 00:31:52,020
when a trap occurs,

1003
00:31:52,020 --> 00:31:52,660
then we know

1004
00:31:52,660 --> 00:31:56,220
we need to bring this up here,

1005
00:31:56,220 --> 00:31:57,900
and that is, when a trap occurs,

1006
00:31:57,900 --> 00:31:58,420
what happens on this side is,

1007
00:31:58,420 --> 00:31:59,760
let me explain this to everyone,

1008
00:31:59,760 --> 00:32:01,680
the question is, why is there no UPP?

1009
00:32:01,680 --> 00:32:02,360
Because, as you can see,

1010
00:32:02,360 --> 00:32:05,560
there are only SPP and MPP here,

1011
00:32:05,560 --> 00:32:07,680
meaning there is only the

1012
00:32:07,680 --> 00:32:09,900
previous privilege in machine mode

1013
00:32:09,900 --> 00:32:11,520
and the

1014
00:32:11,520 --> 00:32:13,060
previous privilege in supervisor mode,

1015
00:32:13,060 --> 00:32:15,400
but no UPP.

1016
00:32:15,400 --> 00:32:17,140
Now, let me explain this to everyone,

1017
00:32:17,140 --> 00:32:18,340
that is, we say that

1018
00:32:18,340 --> 00:32:20,140
when an exception occurs,

1019
00:32:20,140 --> 00:32:22,480
there is actually a concept behind it,

1020
00:32:22,480 --> 00:32:24,620
namely, it always goes from this

1021
00:32:24,620 --> 00:32:29,060
That is to say, when an exception occurs,

1022
00:32:29,060 --> 00:32:30,760
its overall privilege,

1023
00:32:30,760 --> 00:32:31,940
this level,

1024
00:32:31,940 --> 00:32:33,700
it always goes from a higher,

1025
00:32:33,700 --> 00:32:36,720
from a lower privilege level to a higher privilege level,

1026
00:32:36,720 --> 00:32:38,520
and never the other way around.

1027
00:32:38,520 --> 00:32:39,820
To give you an example,

1028
00:32:39,820 --> 00:32:40,800
let's say,

1029
00:32:40,800 --> 00:32:42,780
you are currently working in user mode,

1030
00:32:42,780 --> 00:32:46,000
then while in this user mode,

1031
00:32:46,000 --> 00:32:47,340
say an exception is triggered,

1032
00:32:47,340 --> 00:32:49,800
for instance, you execute an illegal instruction,

1033
00:32:49,800 --> 00:32:51,160
or an interrupt comes in,

1034
00:32:51,160 --> 00:32:52,180
then at that moment,

1035
00:32:52,180 --> 00:32:53,180
the CPU,

1036
00:32:53,180 --> 00:32:54,380
it will jump from user mode

1037
00:32:54,380 --> 00:32:56,660
into M mode.

1038
00:32:56,660 --> 00:32:58,580
We'll cover this later on,

1039
00:32:58,580 --> 00:33:00,960
it always jumps into M mode,

1040
00:33:00,960 --> 00:33:02,440
but it won't just

1041
00:33:02,440 --> 00:33:04,160
When an interrupt occurs in M-mode,

1042
00:33:04,160 --> 00:33:04,920
it jumps back.

1043
00:33:04,920 --> 00:33:05,860
That is impossible.

1044
00:33:05,860 --> 00:33:07,140
That's impossible.

1045
00:33:07,140 --> 00:33:07,800
So think about it,

1046
00:33:07,800 --> 00:33:09,100
since it is

1047
00:33:09,100 --> 00:33:11,720
this kind of

1048
00:33:11,720 --> 00:33:13,140
since it has this kind of effect,

1049
00:33:13,140 --> 00:33:14,180
then it's a downward

1050
00:33:14,180 --> 00:33:16,740
you can think of it as

1051
00:33:16,740 --> 00:33:17,860
the top is

1052
00:33:17,860 --> 00:33:19,740
the one above is below

1053
00:33:19,740 --> 00:33:21,180
so since this goes downward,

1054
00:33:21,180 --> 00:33:22,400
it's the concept of falling into a trap.

1055
00:33:22,400 --> 00:33:24,200
So this is what a trap means here.

1056
00:33:24,200 --> 00:33:25,900
That's where this meaning comes from.

1057
00:33:25,900 --> 00:33:27,140
trap literally means a pitfall.

1058
00:33:27,140 --> 00:33:28,460
It means falling down.

1059
00:33:28,460 --> 00:33:29,540
So this

1060
00:33:29,540 --> 00:33:33,400
always goes from a lower privilege level

1061
00:33:33,400 --> 00:33:34,800
to a higher privilege level.

1062
00:33:34,800 --> 00:33:36,600
Because usually when we draw diagrams,

1063
00:33:36,600 --> 00:33:37,040
we always put

1064
00:33:37,040 --> 00:33:37,920
say, kernel mode

1065
00:33:37,920 --> 00:33:38,720
always at the bottom,

1066
00:33:38,720 --> 00:33:40,220
and user mode at the top.

1067
00:33:40,220 --> 00:33:41,220
So it is always a

1068
00:33:41,220 --> 00:33:44,500
switch from top to bottom,

1069
00:33:44,500 --> 00:33:45,140
a process like that.

1070
00:33:45,140 --> 00:33:46,680
That is why this is called a trap.

1071
00:33:46,680 --> 00:33:48,460
Now, maybe

1072
00:33:48,460 --> 00:33:48,940
having reached this point,

1073
00:33:48,940 --> 00:33:49,720
you may wonder why

1074
00:33:49,720 --> 00:33:50,840
it is called a trap,

1075
00:33:50,840 --> 00:33:51,640
and that is exactly where the name comes from.

1076
00:33:51,640 --> 00:33:55,020
So this way,

1077
00:33:55,020 --> 00:33:57,580
we can go a step further to understand.

1078
00:33:57,580 --> 00:33:58,980
That is to say, as we just mentioned,

1079
00:33:58,980 --> 00:33:59,980
it can only be from U to M,

1080
00:33:59,980 --> 00:34:04,710
so trapping into this M mode

1081
00:34:04,710 --> 00:34:05,770
How many possibilities are there?

1082
00:34:05,770 --> 00:34:07,410
One is U to M.

1083
00:34:07,410 --> 00:34:09,390
It can also be S to M.

1084
00:34:09,390 --> 00:34:11,610
And then there's another one, M to M.

1085
00:34:11,610 --> 00:34:13,730
That means it was already running in M mode,

1086
00:34:13,730 --> 00:34:16,230
and then an interrupt occurs,

1087
00:34:16,230 --> 00:34:18,350
so it still enters M mode.

1088
00:34:18,350 --> 00:34:20,810
So for this M

1089
00:34:20,810 --> 00:34:22,230
MPP,

1090
00:34:22,230 --> 00:34:23,530
there are three possibilities.

1091
00:34:23,530 --> 00:34:25,070
And since there are three possibilities,

1092
00:34:25,070 --> 00:34:27,970
we need at least two bits to represent them,

1093
00:34:27,970 --> 00:34:28,590
right?

1094
00:34:28,590 --> 00:34:31,370
Because two bits can represent up to four cases.

1095
00:34:31,370 --> 00:34:33,230
Since we have three cases to represent,

1096
00:34:33,230 --> 00:34:35,810
that's why this field needs two bits.

1097
00:34:35,810 --> 00:34:38,030
Why do we use two bits to represent it?

1098
00:34:38,030 --> 00:34:38,730
That's exactly the reason.

1099
00:34:38,730 --> 00:34:40,690
Now, for S mode,

1100
00:34:40,690 --> 00:34:43,450
does anyone know when we would trap into S mode?

1101
00:34:43,450 --> 00:34:45,810
So there are only two possibilities.

1102
00:34:45,810 --> 00:34:48,690
Either it switched over from U,

1103
00:34:48,690 --> 00:34:50,510
or S switched over by itself.

1104
00:34:50,510 --> 00:34:50,950
Right?

1105
00:34:50,950 --> 00:34:52,650
We say it can't have come from M,

1106
00:34:52,650 --> 00:34:55,150
because you can't go from high to low.

1107
00:34:55,150 --> 00:34:57,870
So, since there are only two possibilities,

1108
00:34:57,870 --> 00:35:00,350
we only need a single bit to represent them here.

1109
00:35:00,350 --> 00:35:01,830
0 and 1 can represent them, right?

1110
00:35:01,830 --> 00:35:04,050
Because look here, U is 0,

1111
00:35:04,050 --> 00:35:06,610
and S is 1.

1112
00:35:06,610 --> 00:35:09,170
So for S, there are only

1113
00:35:09,170 --> 00:35:10,490
its previous mode,

1114
00:35:10,490 --> 00:35:13,430
the type of the previous mode before the switch,

1115
00:35:13,430 --> 00:35:14,710
there can only be two.

1116
00:35:14,710 --> 00:35:15,270
It's either U,

1117
00:35:15,270 --> 00:35:16,250
or it's S, right?

1118
00:35:16,250 --> 00:35:17,610
It can't be M.

1119
00:35:17,610 --> 00:35:19,390
Now as for U,

1120
00:35:19,390 --> 00:35:20,470
since we said

1121
00:35:20,470 --> 00:35:21,610
As for its trap,

1122
00:35:21,610 --> 00:35:24,370
it can only trap into itself.

1123
00:35:24,370 --> 00:35:26,330
So since that's the case,

1124
00:35:26,330 --> 00:35:27,170
there's actually no need for us to

1125
00:35:27,170 --> 00:35:29,730
use a dedicated bit to represent

1126
00:35:29,730 --> 00:35:30,430
its previous one,

1127
00:35:30,430 --> 00:35:31,950
because the one before it can only be itself.

1128
00:35:31,950 --> 00:35:32,610
Think about it — isn't that right?

1129
00:35:32,610 --> 00:35:35,070
So we save where we can.

1130
00:35:35,070 --> 00:35:39,010
When designing this register, we have to be frugal,

1131
00:35:39,010 --> 00:35:41,110
because every bit is very precious.

1132
00:35:41,110 --> 00:35:42,310
So as you can see,

1133
00:35:42,310 --> 00:35:44,910
this UPP is not needed,

1134
00:35:44,910 --> 00:35:45,810
and that's the reason why.

1135
00:35:45,810 --> 00:35:46,750
Moving on to here,

1136
00:35:46,750 --> 00:35:49,050
it actually also involves this TRAP —

1137
00:35:49,050 --> 00:35:50,970
what kind of process it really is.

1138
00:35:50,970 --> 00:35:53,370
In that case,

1139
00:35:53,370 --> 00:35:54,510
as we know,

1140
00:35:54,510 --> 00:35:56,170
this mstatus register,

1141
00:35:56,170 --> 00:35:58,170
It actually contains a lot of information.

1142
00:35:58,170 --> 00:36:00,190
It can be used to control whether interrupts are enabled.

1143
00:36:00,190 --> 00:36:03,110
It can also indicate some status of the interrupt.

1144
00:36:03,110 --> 00:36:05,370
As well as the interrupt status before the interrupt occurred.

1145
00:36:05,370 --> 00:36:08,070
As well as, before the interrupt or exception occurred,

1146
00:36:08,070 --> 00:36:10,550
the privilege level information.

1147
00:36:10,550 --> 00:36:13,050
We will use these later on.

1148
00:36:13,050 --> 00:36:13,930
So,

1149
00:36:13,930 --> 00:36:15,290
this register is also very important.

1150
00:36:15,290 --> 00:36:19,190
This is mstatus.

1151
00:36:19,190 --> 00:36:23,150
So, for the design of the registers,

1152
00:36:23,150 --> 00:36:24,890
we'll just stop our brief introduction here.

1153
00:36:24,890 --> 00:36:26,390
Next,

1154
00:36:26,390 --> 00:36:29,810
let's take a look at the trap handling flow.

1155
00:36:29,810 --> 00:36:31,450
The trap handling flow.

1156
00:36:31,450 --> 00:36:37,710
As for the trap handling flow,

1157
00:36:37,710 --> 00:36:38,330
it's actually quite simple.

1158
00:36:38,330 --> 00:36:39,650
That is, first of all,

1159
00:36:39,650 --> 00:36:41,070
we need to initialize it.

1160
00:36:41,070 --> 00:36:43,070
Initialize

1161
00:36:43,070 --> 00:36:44,770
The initialization process

1162
00:36:44,770 --> 00:36:45,570
actually includes

1163
00:36:45,570 --> 00:36:48,190
for example, setting up its entry address

1164
00:36:48,190 --> 00:36:49,410
and other related concepts.

1165
00:36:49,410 --> 00:36:50,730
Then,

1166
00:36:50,730 --> 00:36:52,930
the second stage

1167
00:36:52,930 --> 00:36:55,650
is what we call the top half,

1168
00:36:55,650 --> 00:36:58,510
the upper part of the trap.

1169
00:36:58,510 --> 00:37:00,130
Now, the top half of the trap

1170
00:37:00,130 --> 00:37:00,910
is essentially saying that,

1171
00:37:00,910 --> 00:37:02,910
the actual process of it happening

1172
00:37:02,910 --> 00:37:07,010
takes place inside the hardware.

1173
00:37:07,010 --> 00:37:09,590
It is a processing flow inside the hardware.

1174
00:37:09,590 --> 00:37:11,390
It is not something we control.

1175
00:37:11,390 --> 00:37:12,610
It is a hardware processing flow.

1176
00:37:12,610 --> 00:37:14,450
So after this hardware processing flow

1177
00:37:14,450 --> 00:37:15,470
is completed,

1178
00:37:15,470 --> 00:37:18,070
only then does it jump to our

1179
00:37:18,070 --> 00:37:20,210
software part.

1180
00:37:20,210 --> 00:37:22,710
This is what we call

1181
00:37:22,710 --> 00:37:24,370
The bottom half of this trap,

1182
00:37:24,370 --> 00:37:26,690
so the bottom half of this trap,

1183
00:37:26,690 --> 00:37:27,430
this part in blue,

1184
00:37:27,430 --> 00:37:29,130
you can think of it as a piece of

1185
00:37:29,130 --> 00:37:30,150
program logic that we wrote.

1186
00:37:30,150 --> 00:37:34,030
Whereas the top half just now,

1187
00:37:34,030 --> 00:37:35,790
actually happens inside the hardware CPU,

1188
00:37:35,790 --> 00:37:37,310
so it is not under our control.

1189
00:37:37,310 --> 00:37:39,990
Then this bottom half part,

1190
00:37:39,990 --> 00:37:41,050
that is the logic we execute.

1191
00:37:41,050 --> 00:37:42,470
After that execution is done,

1192
00:37:42,470 --> 00:37:43,510
please note that,

1193
00:37:43,510 --> 00:37:44,750
there is one last thing, that is,

1194
00:37:44,750 --> 00:37:45,430
there must be a return.

1195
00:37:45,430 --> 00:37:47,330
Because as we have emphasized many times before,

1196
00:37:47,330 --> 00:37:49,110
in the execution process of this TRAP,

1197
00:37:49,110 --> 00:37:51,910
it has a return step,

1198
00:37:51,910 --> 00:37:55,230
that is, it returns to the original normal control flow.

1199
00:37:55,230 --> 00:37:58,070
So its whole processing flow,

1200
00:37:58,070 --> 00:37:59,870
we can simply summarize it as

1201
00:37:59,870 --> 00:38:00,970
These four major steps

1202
00:38:00,970 --> 00:38:02,930
So next, let's take a look

1203
00:38:02,930 --> 00:38:03,510
and look in detail

1204
00:38:03,510 --> 00:38:04,830
at what happens in the process of these four major steps

1205
00:38:04,830 --> 00:38:07,810
what exactly we need to do

1206
00:38:07,810 --> 00:38:10,550
or, in other words, what actually happens

1207
00:38:10,550 --> 00:38:12,110
Let's start with the first one

1208
00:38:12,110 --> 00:38:13,770
which is trap initialization

1209
00:38:13,770 --> 00:38:15,550
Now, as for the trap initialization,

1210
00:38:15,550 --> 00:38:16,710
it's actually very simple

1211
00:38:16,710 --> 00:38:18,690
Combined with what we just covered

1212
00:38:18,690 --> 00:38:22,050
about these registers,

1213
00:38:22,050 --> 00:38:23,470
the first step is actually

1214
00:38:23,470 --> 00:38:25,970
to set the entry function

1215
00:38:25,970 --> 00:38:27,350
which is our

1216
00:38:27,350 --> 00:38:29,550
bottom half code,

1217
00:38:29,550 --> 00:38:30,950
its starting address

1218
00:38:30,950 --> 00:38:31,910
because this address

1219
00:38:31,910 --> 00:38:33,450
needs to be told to our CPU,

1220
00:38:33,450 --> 00:38:35,830
so that when a trap occurs on this chip,

1221
00:38:35,830 --> 00:38:37,190
it will only then jump to this address

1222
00:38:37,190 --> 00:38:38,530
to execute the second half of our handler.

1223
00:38:38,530 --> 00:38:39,850
Does everyone understand that?

1224
00:38:39,850 --> 00:38:41,930
So we need to tell it

1225
00:38:41,930 --> 00:38:42,510
this address,

1226
00:38:42,510 --> 00:38:44,110
and this address is what we call the base

1227
00:38:44,110 --> 00:38:44,750
address.

1228
00:38:44,750 --> 00:38:45,850
So this base address,

1229
00:38:45,850 --> 00:38:46,890
how do we tell it to the hardware?

1230
00:38:46,890 --> 00:38:49,750
We actually need to set

1231
00:38:49,750 --> 00:38:51,250
the register I just introduced to you,

1232
00:38:51,250 --> 00:38:53,770
the mtvec register.

1233
00:38:53,770 --> 00:38:55,090
Now this

1234
00:38:55,090 --> 00:38:56,990
is something I extracted from the code.

1235
00:38:56,990 --> 00:38:58,050
This is actually a

1236
00:38:58,050 --> 00:38:59,650
it's a function,

1237
00:38:59,650 --> 00:39:00,690
it's a function.

1238
00:39:00,690 --> 00:39:02,370
How exactly it is

1239
00:39:02,370 --> 00:39:03,610
operated inside this function,

1240
00:39:03,610 --> 00:39:04,470
we'll go look at it ourselves.

1241
00:39:04,470 --> 00:39:05,570
I won't go into that again.

1242
00:39:05,570 --> 00:39:06,950
That is to say,

1243
00:39:06,950 --> 00:39:09,190
what this actually means is

1244
00:39:09,190 --> 00:39:10,350
we need to write this,

1245
00:39:10,350 --> 00:39:14,270
write the MTVC register,

1246
00:39:14,270 --> 00:39:17,550
and set inside this register our

1247
00:39:17,550 --> 00:39:20,410
base address of this entry address,

1248
00:39:20,410 --> 00:39:21,590
that is, the base address.

1249
00:39:21,590 --> 00:39:22,710
Now, here you can see

1250
00:39:22,710 --> 00:39:23,730
this entry address,

1251
00:39:23,730 --> 00:39:25,850
this function is actually a piece of assembly logic

1252
00:39:25,850 --> 00:39:28,730
that we wrote in entry.s, the assembly file.

1253
00:39:28,730 --> 00:39:30,190
Now, this assembly logic,

1254
00:39:30,190 --> 00:39:31,090
as we mentioned before,

1255
00:39:31,090 --> 00:39:32,710
in the syntax of this assembly,

1256
00:39:32,710 --> 00:39:34,170
this label is actually this,

1257
00:39:34,170 --> 00:39:35,570
it's actually just an address.

1258
00:39:35,570 --> 00:39:36,130
Think about it, isn't that right?

1259
00:39:36,130 --> 00:39:37,570
This label, in fact, in terms of

1260
00:39:37,570 --> 00:39:39,150
the concept of relocatable linking,

1261
00:39:39,150 --> 00:39:40,050
It is actually just an address.

1262
00:39:40,050 --> 00:39:41,130
So we take this label,

1263
00:39:41,130 --> 00:39:42,650
fill the label into this place,

1264
00:39:42,650 --> 00:39:44,850
which is essentially putting the address of this function

1265
00:39:44,850 --> 00:39:47,370
into this calculator.

1266
00:39:47,370 --> 00:39:48,670
That's its initialization.

1267
00:39:48,670 --> 00:39:49,290
Very simple.

1268
00:39:49,290 --> 00:39:53,230
Now for the second part,

1269
00:39:53,230 --> 00:39:54,450
we say it's the top half.

1270
00:39:54,450 --> 00:39:55,090
The top half,

1271
00:39:55,090 --> 00:39:57,910
this part is actually done by the hardware.

1272
00:39:57,910 --> 00:40:00,550
Our software part is not involved here.

1273
00:40:00,550 --> 00:40:02,870
But we need to understand what this part is doing,

1274
00:40:02,870 --> 00:40:05,050
so that we can better understand

1275
00:40:05,050 --> 00:40:07,230
what our bottom half should do.

1276
00:40:07,230 --> 00:40:09,010
So let's first take a look

1277
00:40:09,010 --> 00:40:10,450
at what this top half does.

1278
00:40:10,450 --> 00:40:12,030
So for the top half,

1279
00:40:12,030 --> 00:40:16,130
we say the hardware automatically performs the following state transitions,

1280
00:40:16,130 --> 00:40:17,850
meaning this is what the CPU does.

1281
00:40:17,850 --> 00:40:20,130
So let's look at what it does.

1282
00:40:20,130 --> 00:40:21,450
Its first step

1283
00:40:21,450 --> 00:40:26,790
is that it copies the MIE value of mstatus into MPIE.

1284
00:40:26,790 --> 00:40:27,670
Now, what does that mean?

1285
00:40:27,670 --> 00:40:31,810
We mentioned earlier that the concept of MIE is that

1286
00:40:31,810 --> 00:40:36,210
when this trap occurs,

1287
00:40:36,210 --> 00:40:39,690
the state of our interrupt enable bit

1288
00:40:39,690 --> 00:40:41,330
tells us whether interrupts are currently on or off.

1289
00:40:41,330 --> 00:40:44,410
It copies that value into here.

1290
00:40:44,410 --> 00:40:47,630
Copying it must be because it will be used later.

1291
00:40:47,630 --> 00:40:48,510
Let's set that aside for now.

1292
00:40:48,510 --> 00:40:51,930
Then it clears its MIE flag.

1293
00:40:51,930 --> 00:40:52,730
Now, as you know,

1294
00:40:52,730 --> 00:40:54,670
we know that this MIE flag,

1295
00:40:54,670 --> 00:40:56,790
if you just write a 0 into it,

1296
00:40:56,790 --> 00:40:58,930
that actually means interrupts are disabled.

1297
00:40:58,930 --> 00:41:00,310
So this action,

1298
00:41:00,310 --> 00:41:01,890
this first action of the CPU,

1299
00:41:01,890 --> 00:41:02,990
is actually quite clear.

1300
00:41:02,990 --> 00:41:03,850
It is telling us what?

1301
00:41:03,850 --> 00:41:06,090
That is to say, when we go and execute

1302
00:41:06,090 --> 00:41:07,290
the second half of its work,

1303
00:41:07,290 --> 00:41:08,670
our CPU is actually in a state where

1304
00:41:08,670 --> 00:41:10,670
interrupts have been disabled.

1305
00:41:10,670 --> 00:41:12,770
Because at this point, even if an interrupt occurs,

1306
00:41:12,770 --> 00:41:15,010
it won't come and interrupt us.

1307
00:41:15,010 --> 00:41:17,030
This concept is very important.

1308
00:41:17,030 --> 00:41:21,650
So what this step achieves is that interrupts are disabled.

1309
00:41:21,650 --> 00:41:23,650
That's the first action.

1310
00:41:23,650 --> 00:41:25,670
Now, what does the CPU do as the second action?

1311
00:41:25,670 --> 00:41:27,830
It will set MEPC,

1312
00:41:27,830 --> 00:41:32,790
and at the same time, PC will be set to this value here.

1313
00:41:32,790 --> 00:41:36,390
Now, this setting of MEPC,

1314
00:41:36,390 --> 00:41:39,970
is actually also for use when we return later.

1315
00:41:39,970 --> 00:41:42,030
Because we know that this MEPC is essentially

1316
00:41:42,030 --> 00:41:43,170
where, when we return,

1317
00:41:43,170 --> 00:41:44,490
when we execute mret,

1318
00:41:44,490 --> 00:41:46,650
it will read out the corresponding value from here,

1319
00:41:46,650 --> 00:41:48,230
and then jump back.

1320
00:41:48,230 --> 00:41:50,010
Now, as for the setting,

1321
00:41:50,010 --> 00:41:51,750
So, as for the specific setting,

1322
00:41:51,750 --> 00:41:53,050
the specific setting,

1323
00:41:53,050 --> 00:41:54,930
does it set the PC value

1324
00:41:54,930 --> 00:41:56,290
or the value of the next instruction? Well,

1325
00:41:56,290 --> 00:41:57,610
it will depend on this:

1326
00:41:57,610 --> 00:41:59,330
whether what we currently have is

1327
00:41:59,330 --> 00:42:02,450
an exception or an interrupt, and handle them differently.

1328
00:42:02,450 --> 00:42:03,730
We've written this out for you as well,

1329
00:42:03,730 --> 00:42:05,330
meaning that if it is an exception,

1330
00:42:05,330 --> 00:42:08,630
then the MPC points to the instruction that caused the exception,

1331
00:42:08,630 --> 00:42:10,430
whereas if it is an interrupt,

1332
00:42:10,430 --> 00:42:12,250
it points to the instruction after the interrupt.

1333
00:42:12,250 --> 00:42:13,750
What does this mean? Well,

1334
00:42:13,750 --> 00:42:15,290
we've drawn the two figures below for you,

1335
00:42:15,290 --> 00:42:15,990
so let's work through them.

1336
00:42:15,990 --> 00:42:19,970
The meaning expressed by the figure on the left

1337
00:42:19,970 --> 00:42:21,550
is that when an exception occurs,

1338
00:42:21,550 --> 00:42:25,790
for example, while it is executing this instruction,

1339
00:42:25,790 --> 00:42:28,130
this instruction instruction2,

1340
00:42:28,130 --> 00:42:31,670
say this instruction is an exception instruction,

1341
00:42:31,670 --> 00:42:33,290
You made a mistake when you compiled it

1342
00:42:33,290 --> 00:42:34,950
You wrote an exception instruction

1343
00:42:34,950 --> 00:42:36,610
An instruction we don't recognize got written in there

1344
00:42:36,610 --> 00:42:37,670
But if it's assembled at compile time,

1345
00:42:37,670 --> 00:42:38,490
it will definitely tell you

1346
00:42:38,490 --> 00:42:39,730
Suppose we hard-code it,

1347
00:42:39,730 --> 00:42:41,310
and we just write an exception instruction,

1348
00:42:41,310 --> 00:42:42,310
or say we write something that

1349
00:42:42,310 --> 00:42:44,410
accesses something it shouldn't access,

1350
00:42:44,410 --> 00:42:46,130
all of these are possible,

1351
00:42:46,130 --> 00:42:47,610
triggering an exception.

1352
00:42:47,610 --> 00:42:49,150
Now, when this instruction is executed,

1353
00:42:49,150 --> 00:42:50,030
assume this PC,

1354
00:42:50,030 --> 00:42:51,770
the PC is currently pointing here.

1355
00:42:51,770 --> 00:42:53,730
So the PC is likely the address of this instruction.

1356
00:42:53,730 --> 00:42:56,690
Now, when an exception is triggered,

1357
00:42:56,690 --> 00:42:59,810
it will save the value of this PC

1358
00:42:59,810 --> 00:43:01,410
into mepc.

1359
00:43:01,410 --> 00:43:04,750
And once it's saved there,

1360
00:43:04,750 --> 00:43:06,170
as you know, when it triggers an exception,

1361
00:43:06,170 --> 00:43:07,770
it goes off to execute an exception handler.

1362
00:43:07,770 --> 00:43:08,930
After the exception is handled,

1363
00:43:08,930 --> 00:43:10,310
it will come back.

1364
00:43:10,310 --> 00:43:11,390
Now, when it comes back,

1365
00:43:11,390 --> 00:43:12,570
it actually returns based on this,

1366
00:43:12,570 --> 00:43:15,150
based on the MEPC you saved.

1367
00:43:15,150 --> 00:43:17,490
So, since when you jumped away,

1368
00:43:17,490 --> 00:43:20,070
what you saved was the value of this instruction,

1369
00:43:20,070 --> 00:43:21,290
then think about it — when it comes back,

1370
00:43:21,290 --> 00:43:22,530
it actually returns to this same instruction,

1371
00:43:22,530 --> 00:43:24,230
and starts executing this instruction again.

1372
00:43:24,230 --> 00:43:26,450
So that is actually the effect an exception has.

1373
00:43:26,450 --> 00:43:28,590
Now, there is definitely a reason for doing it this way.

1374
00:43:28,590 --> 00:43:30,470
Because if you think about it,

1375
00:43:30,470 --> 00:43:31,830
for example, we...

1376
00:43:31,830 --> 00:43:35,110
what is it actually trying to achieve here?

1377
00:43:35,110 --> 00:43:37,710
That is to say, right when this exception occurs,

1378
00:43:37,710 --> 00:43:39,390
it gives us an opportunity —

1379
00:43:39,390 --> 00:43:40,150
from the programmer's,

1380
00:43:40,150 --> 00:43:41,530
from the CPU's perspective —

1381
00:43:41,530 --> 00:43:42,310
You can actually understand it this way:

1382
00:43:42,310 --> 00:43:42,610
that is to say,

1383
00:43:42,610 --> 00:43:44,750
when it executes this instruction,

1384
00:43:44,750 --> 00:43:46,090
it decides that this instruction

1385
00:43:46,090 --> 00:43:48,210
has triggered some abnormal behavior,

1386
00:43:48,210 --> 00:43:49,410
and so it goes

1387
00:43:49,410 --> 00:43:50,970
and gives us a chance.

1388
00:43:50,970 --> 00:43:51,590
This chance

1389
00:43:51,590 --> 00:43:52,430
is the programmer's.

1390
00:43:52,430 --> 00:43:53,870
When the programmer gets this chance,

1391
00:43:53,870 --> 00:43:54,490
they go and write an

1392
00:43:54,490 --> 00:43:55,770
exception handler function.

1393
00:43:55,770 --> 00:43:56,290
This,

1394
00:43:56,290 --> 00:43:57,910
it expects you to, within this

1395
00:43:57,910 --> 00:43:59,110
exception handler function,

1396
00:43:59,110 --> 00:44:00,490
take this exception,

1397
00:44:00,490 --> 00:44:01,430
this problem that was triggered,

1398
00:44:01,430 --> 00:44:02,210
and solve it.

1399
00:44:02,210 --> 00:44:03,490
After it's solved,

1400
00:44:03,490 --> 00:44:04,370
then it comes back,

1401
00:44:04,370 --> 00:44:05,070
So it's like saying,

1402
00:44:05,070 --> 00:44:06,170
it gives you one more

1403
00:44:06,170 --> 00:44:08,110
chance to retry

1404
00:44:08,110 --> 00:44:09,310
and execute it once again.

1405
00:44:09,310 --> 00:44:10,190
Then it's possible that

1406
00:44:10,190 --> 00:44:11,450
when handling this exception,

1407
00:44:11,450 --> 00:44:12,570
it will actually fix

1408
00:44:12,570 --> 00:44:14,510
the problem that caused

1409
00:44:14,510 --> 00:44:16,350
the exception in the first place.

1410
00:44:16,350 --> 00:44:17,170
So,

1411
00:44:17,170 --> 00:44:18,630
this is what we call an exception.

1412
00:44:18,630 --> 00:44:20,330
So an exception,

1413
00:44:20,330 --> 00:44:21,250
what it actually interrupts is

1414
00:44:21,250 --> 00:44:22,670
the current instruction.

1415
00:44:22,670 --> 00:44:25,250
After it's done,

1416
00:44:25,250 --> 00:44:26,270
it will come back,

1417
00:44:26,270 --> 00:44:27,810
and re-execute the instruction

1418
00:44:27,810 --> 00:44:28,870
that triggered the exception.

1419
00:44:28,870 --> 00:44:31,670
If we go a bit deeper,

1420
00:44:31,670 --> 00:44:32,570
for example, you might

1421
00:44:32,570 --> 00:44:33,430
If you've also learned about

1422
00:44:33,430 --> 00:44:34,890
for example, a page fault exception,

1423
00:44:34,890 --> 00:44:37,610
which gets triggered all the time in Linux

1424
00:44:37,610 --> 00:44:39,230
this page fault exception,

1425
00:44:39,230 --> 00:44:40,450
actually, think about it,

1426
00:44:40,450 --> 00:44:41,170
a page fault means

1427
00:44:41,170 --> 00:44:43,190
you find that the current instruction

1428
00:44:43,190 --> 00:44:44,470
is not in your memory,

1429
00:44:44,470 --> 00:44:45,210
then it might

1430
00:44:45,210 --> 00:44:46,090
this is the exception being triggered,

1431
00:44:46,090 --> 00:44:47,670
and it will make you do what? Go

1432
00:44:47,670 --> 00:44:48,210
fetch the page,

1433
00:44:48,210 --> 00:44:49,010
swap the page in.

1434
00:44:49,010 --> 00:44:49,870
After the page has been swapped in,

1435
00:44:49,870 --> 00:44:51,230
this instruction is simply re

1436
00:44:51,230 --> 00:44:53,010
if it gets re-executed,

1437
00:44:53,010 --> 00:44:54,530
doesn't that solve the problem?

1438
00:44:54,530 --> 00:44:55,170
Think about it, isn't that the case?

1439
00:44:55,170 --> 00:44:56,670
So this kind of thing is an exception.

1440
00:44:56,670 --> 00:44:58,310
An interrupt, on the other hand,

1441
00:44:58,310 --> 00:44:59,710
That is not the idea here.

1442
00:44:59,710 --> 00:45:00,790
Because with an interrupt,

1443
00:45:00,790 --> 00:45:02,010
the actual intent is,

1444
00:45:02,010 --> 00:45:03,630
what it wants to achieve is this kind of effect:

1445
00:45:03,630 --> 00:45:03,970
that is,

1446
00:45:03,970 --> 00:45:06,030
say it is executing this instruction,

1447
00:45:06,030 --> 00:45:08,350
and an interrupt is triggered.

1448
00:45:08,350 --> 00:45:09,830
Now, an interrupt,

1449
00:45:09,830 --> 00:45:12,650
in fact, is generated by an external peripheral.

1450
00:45:12,650 --> 00:45:15,530
So it has to go and run a piece of logic for that peripheral,

1451
00:45:15,530 --> 00:45:19,010
maybe to read a piece of data from an external device,

1452
00:45:19,010 --> 00:45:20,110
or something like that.

1453
00:45:20,110 --> 00:45:20,310
So,

1454
00:45:20,310 --> 00:45:24,370
when it is handling the interrupt,

1455
00:45:24,370 --> 00:45:26,510
and it saves this MEPC,

1456
00:45:26,510 --> 00:45:29,710
what it actually saves is the address of the next instruction.

1457
00:45:29,710 --> 00:45:31,450
So this way,

1458
00:45:31,450 --> 00:45:32,610
after it finishes this one,

1459
00:45:32,610 --> 00:45:35,070
of course it will finish executing this instruction first,

1460
00:45:35,070 --> 00:45:36,190
and after it finishes,

1461
00:45:36,190 --> 00:45:38,110
It goes off to execute an interrupt handler

1462
00:45:38,110 --> 00:45:40,250
and once that's done, once the handling is finished,

1463
00:45:40,250 --> 00:45:41,710
it comes back.

1464
00:45:41,710 --> 00:45:42,390
And when it comes back,

1465
00:45:42,390 --> 00:45:43,810
it actually returns to the next instruction.

1466
00:45:43,810 --> 00:45:44,650
So in this way,

1467
00:45:44,650 --> 00:45:46,490
the effect it achieves is that,

1468
00:45:46,490 --> 00:45:49,150
from the point of view of our normal execution flow,

1469
00:45:49,150 --> 00:45:50,170
it's basically as if

1470
00:45:50,170 --> 00:45:52,250
nothing ever happened in between.

1471
00:45:52,250 --> 00:45:53,630
Because it's actually an interrupt,

1472
00:45:53,630 --> 00:45:54,470
not an exception.

1473
00:45:54,470 --> 00:45:55,730
Does everyone get this?

1474
00:45:55,730 --> 00:45:56,730
So this interrupt

1475
00:45:56,730 --> 00:45:57,590
actually went and did something else,

1476
00:45:57,590 --> 00:45:59,110
but while it was doing that,

1477
00:45:59,110 --> 00:46:00,350
it didn't want us —

1478
00:46:00,350 --> 00:46:02,810
from the programmer's point of view,

1479
00:46:02,810 --> 00:46:03,690
that is, you should feel

1480
00:46:03,690 --> 00:46:04,290
like this whole thing

1481
00:46:04,290 --> 00:46:05,950
as if nothing ever happened

1482
00:46:05,950 --> 00:46:07,370
So it actually

1483
00:46:07,370 --> 00:46:08,910
when it comes back, it executes

1484
00:46:08,910 --> 00:46:10,930
directly executes the next instruction

1485
00:46:10,930 --> 00:46:11,650
at this point

1486
00:46:11,650 --> 00:46:14,630
So this setup of MEPC

1487
00:46:14,630 --> 00:46:16,750
could be a different value

1488
00:46:16,750 --> 00:46:19,970
Later, when we talk about interrupts,

1489
00:46:19,970 --> 00:46:20,890
I'll explain to you again

1490
00:46:20,890 --> 00:46:21,610
this related concept

1491
00:46:21,610 --> 00:46:23,450
just mentioning this to you here

1492
00:46:23,450 --> 00:46:26,190
So this automatic step

1493
00:46:26,190 --> 00:46:27,690
the second step is setting MEPC,

1494
00:46:27,690 --> 00:46:30,010
and at the same time, PC is set to this

1495
00:46:30,010 --> 00:46:30,770
Now, knowing this,

1496
00:46:30,770 --> 00:46:32,910
once you know that PC is set to this,

1497
00:46:32,910 --> 00:46:35,590
it actually achieves this jump

1498
00:46:35,590 --> 00:46:36,990
it actually performs the jump

1499
00:46:36,990 --> 00:46:41,240
jumping to the exception handler to execute

1500
00:46:41,240 --> 00:46:44,750
And then this

1501
00:46:44,750 --> 00:46:50,100
The next step,

1502
00:46:50,100 --> 00:46:52,340
it sets mcause according to the type of this trap.

1503
00:46:52,340 --> 00:46:53,380
There's nothing much to say about this.

1504
00:46:53,380 --> 00:46:55,080
It just tells us this:

1505
00:46:55,080 --> 00:46:57,900
the specific exception or interrupt that occurred,

1506
00:46:57,900 --> 00:46:59,020
exactly what kind of exception it is,

1507
00:46:59,020 --> 00:47:00,320
or what kind of interrupt it is.

1508
00:47:00,320 --> 00:47:03,340
Then later, when we go to the bottom half,

1509
00:47:03,340 --> 00:47:05,820
we'll know how to handle it.

1510
00:47:05,820 --> 00:47:07,680
And the last one,

1511
00:47:07,680 --> 00:47:09,480
it saves the privilege mode from before the trap occurred

1512
00:47:09,480 --> 00:47:11,820
in its MPP field.

1513
00:47:11,820 --> 00:47:15,500
This is also used for restoring it later.

1514
00:47:15,500 --> 00:47:16,720
Then,

1515
00:47:16,720 --> 00:47:21,760
it changes the privilege mode to Machine mode.

1516
00:47:21,760 --> 00:47:23,680
What this actually means is that

1517
00:47:23,680 --> 00:47:28,280
no matter which privilege level a trap is triggered at,

1518
00:47:28,280 --> 00:47:32,500
the hart first switches to M-mode to handle it.

1519
00:47:32,500 --> 00:47:33,680
Now, some of you might say,

1520
00:47:33,680 --> 00:47:35,300
doesn't it sometimes also enter

1521
00:47:35,300 --> 00:47:37,180
What about supervisor mode? This one,

1522
00:47:37,180 --> 00:47:37,760
as you can see,

1523
00:47:37,760 --> 00:47:38,640
it is actually implemented through

1524
00:47:38,640 --> 00:47:40,820
a so-called delegation mechanism.

1525
00:47:40,820 --> 00:47:41,320
This is

1526
00:47:41,320 --> 00:47:43,060
not something we use in our course,

1527
00:47:43,060 --> 00:47:44,060
so I will just mention it briefly here.

1528
00:47:44,060 --> 00:47:45,960
But at the very least, we need to know

1529
00:47:45,960 --> 00:47:46,920
that no matter what mode you are in

1530
00:47:46,920 --> 00:47:49,460
at the moment,

1531
00:47:49,460 --> 00:47:51,300
once a trap occurs,

1532
00:47:51,300 --> 00:47:54,000
it will first definitely enter machine mode.

1533
00:47:54,000 --> 00:47:55,780
It enters machine mode first.

1534
00:47:55,780 --> 00:47:56,920
You all need to keep this in mind.

1535
00:47:56,920 --> 00:47:58,820
Alright.

1536
00:47:58,820 --> 00:48:00,980
This is what we call the

1537
00:48:00,980 --> 00:48:03,800
first part of the trap handling process.

1538
00:48:03,800 --> 00:48:05,800
As for this part of the process,

1539
00:48:05,800 --> 00:48:09,120
it is all done automatically by the hardware.

1540
00:48:09,120 --> 00:48:11,520
Then, once the final

1541
00:48:11,520 --> 00:48:13,720
So what's the final, final result?

1542
00:48:13,720 --> 00:48:14,920
Well, it will jump

1543
00:48:14,920 --> 00:48:17,460
— it starts jumping to our

1544
00:48:17,460 --> 00:48:19,960
trap entry function,

1545
00:48:19,960 --> 00:48:21,320
to execute our program.

1546
00:48:21,320 --> 00:48:24,420
So then we enter this second half.

1547
00:48:24,420 --> 00:48:25,620
The second half, as we said,

1548
00:48:25,620 --> 00:48:27,080
is about executing a piece of our program.

1549
00:48:27,080 --> 00:48:28,160
Next,

1550
00:48:28,160 --> 00:48:29,020
let's take a look at

1551
00:48:29,020 --> 00:48:31,120
the third part of trap,

1552
00:48:31,120 --> 00:48:32,400
which is the bottom half.

1553
00:48:32,400 --> 00:48:33,140
The bottom half,

1554
00:48:33,140 --> 00:48:33,720
as the name implies,

1555
00:48:33,720 --> 00:48:34,580
means that this part

1556
00:48:34,580 --> 00:48:35,660
needs to be written

1557
00:48:35,660 --> 00:48:37,840
by us software programmers,

1558
00:48:37,840 --> 00:48:38,720
a trap handler function

1559
00:48:38,720 --> 00:48:40,780
that we write ourselves.

1560
00:48:40,780 --> 00:48:42,800
So today,

1561
00:48:42,800 --> 00:48:43,660
We're mainly talking about exceptions,

1562
00:48:43,660 --> 00:48:44,180
so we

1563
00:48:44,180 --> 00:48:44,980
actually have a piece of

1564
00:48:44,980 --> 00:48:45,940
exception handler code.

1565
00:48:45,940 --> 00:48:47,040
Now,

1566
00:48:47,040 --> 00:48:48,900
we say that whether it's an interrupt

1567
00:48:48,900 --> 00:48:50,200
or an exception,

1568
00:48:50,200 --> 00:48:50,900
as a

1569
00:48:50,900 --> 00:48:52,500
unified trap,

1570
00:48:52,500 --> 00:48:54,060
what should it actually do?

1571
00:48:54,060 --> 00:48:54,720
Well,

1572
00:48:54,720 --> 00:48:56,560
we can actually

1573
00:48:56,560 --> 00:48:57,300
take the entire process

1574
00:48:57,300 --> 00:48:58,020
and summarize it

1575
00:48:58,020 --> 00:48:59,400
roughly into the following five steps.

1576
00:48:59,400 --> 00:49:01,140
So the first step,

1577
00:49:01,140 --> 00:49:03,440
we say, is to save

1578
00:49:03,440 --> 00:49:05,820
the control flow

1579
00:49:05,820 --> 00:49:07,340
context information.

1580
00:49:07,340 --> 00:49:10,700
Now this concept of context

1581
00:49:10,700 --> 00:49:12,240
In the last class, when we were talking about

1582
00:49:12,240 --> 00:49:13,760
context switching,

1583
00:49:13,760 --> 00:49:14,860
we already covered this, right?

1584
00:49:14,860 --> 00:49:15,540
That context,

1585
00:49:15,540 --> 00:49:16,100
in fact, is just

1586
00:49:16,100 --> 00:49:19,280
when this exception occurs,

1587
00:49:19,280 --> 00:49:21,640
on its processor,

1588
00:49:21,640 --> 00:49:22,620
what's in the hardware --

1589
00:49:22,620 --> 00:49:23,920
you can think of it as

1590
00:49:23,920 --> 00:49:25,800
the values of those general-purpose registers.

1591
00:49:25,800 --> 00:49:27,480
So in fact,

1592
00:49:27,480 --> 00:49:30,940
x0, we don't need to save,

1593
00:49:30,940 --> 00:49:31,220
right?

1594
00:49:31,220 --> 00:49:33,540
So x1 through x31 --

1595
00:49:33,540 --> 00:49:35,180
the values of these registers,

1596
00:49:35,180 --> 00:49:36,200
we save them.

1597
00:49:36,200 --> 00:49:37,600
That's what we call saving

1598
00:49:37,600 --> 00:49:38,700
the context information.

1599
00:49:38,700 --> 00:49:42,140
As for mscratch,

1600
00:49:42,140 --> 00:49:42,940
we also mentioned it earlier.

1601
00:49:42,940 --> 00:49:43,940
That is to say, we use this

1602
00:49:43,940 --> 00:49:45,320
this register,

1603
00:49:45,320 --> 00:49:46,000
we actually use it

1604
00:49:46,000 --> 00:49:48,140
to save this.

1605
00:49:48,140 --> 00:49:49,180
This is actually also

1606
00:49:49,180 --> 00:49:50,980
in mode mode, this

1607
00:49:50,980 --> 00:49:52,280
Trap-related register.

1608
00:49:52,280 --> 00:49:53,260
We use this register

1609
00:49:53,260 --> 00:49:54,280
to save this,

1610
00:49:54,280 --> 00:49:55,600
the current

1611
00:49:55,600 --> 00:49:59,520
memory pointing to the current context,

1612
00:49:59,520 --> 00:50:03,940
so we use it to save its information.

1613
00:50:03,940 --> 00:50:05,340
And correspondingly, on our side here,

1614
00:50:05,340 --> 00:50:07,220
correspondingly, our

1615
00:50:07,220 --> 00:50:09,660
right side is actually some sample code.

1616
00:50:09,660 --> 00:50:11,260
You can take a look at this code yourselves.

1617
00:50:11,260 --> 00:50:13,280
And actually it's reflected right here,

1618
00:50:13,280 --> 00:50:15,380
it's this piece of logic.

1619
00:50:15,380 --> 00:50:17,840
Now, this piece of logic,

1620
00:50:17,840 --> 00:50:21,700
what we actually do at its core is call a

1621
00:50:21,700 --> 00:50:24,140
REG underline Save

1622
00:50:24,140 --> 00:50:28,020
This is a macro for register save

1623
00:50:28,020 --> 00:50:31,200
This macro is defined in the assembly like this

1624
00:50:31,200 --> 00:50:32,020
Now, this macro we're writing here

1625
00:50:32,020 --> 00:50:33,340
we introduced to you earlier

1626
00:50:33,340 --> 00:50:35,100
Now, this is actually a parameter

1627
00:50:35,100 --> 00:50:36,760
In other words, the macro takes a parameter

1628
00:50:36,760 --> 00:50:38,640
You can understand this parameter as

1629
00:50:38,640 --> 00:50:41,240
it is actually the value

1630
00:50:41,240 --> 00:50:42,440
swapped out of mscratch

1631
00:50:42,440 --> 00:50:43,260
So what it actually is

1632
00:50:43,260 --> 00:50:45,820
is a pointer to the base address of a piece of memory

1633
00:50:45,820 --> 00:50:47,120
the base address of the memory

1634
00:50:47,120 --> 00:50:48,420
and inside this piece of memory

1635
00:50:48,420 --> 00:50:49,860
is where the context is stored

1636
00:50:49,860 --> 00:50:51,140
Now, within the context

1637
00:50:51,140 --> 00:50:53,160
starting from x1

1638
00:50:53,160 --> 00:50:54,920
all the way to x31

1639
00:50:54,920 --> 00:50:56,200
these registers

1640
00:50:56,200 --> 00:50:57,480
are placed in this context

1641
00:50:57,480 --> 00:50:59,040
So we just take from the CPU

1642
00:50:59,040 --> 00:51:00,180
these data

1643
00:51:00,180 --> 00:51:02,080
and by saving,

1644
00:51:02,080 --> 00:51:04,180
what we mean is taking the values on the CPU,

1645
00:51:04,180 --> 00:51:05,260
the values in the CPU registers,

1646
00:51:05,260 --> 00:51:06,960
and saving them into here

1647
00:51:06,960 --> 00:51:09,980
so that's the save

1648
00:51:09,980 --> 00:51:11,020
And naturally, corresponding to the save,

1649
00:51:11,020 --> 00:51:12,400
what that means is that

1650
00:51:12,400 --> 00:51:13,540
once the processing is done,

1651
00:51:13,540 --> 00:51:14,460
there has to be a restore, right?

1652
00:51:14,460 --> 00:51:15,640
So let me mention this in advance,

1653
00:51:15,640 --> 00:51:16,680
the restore

1654
00:51:16,680 --> 00:51:17,640
is done right here,

1655
00:51:17,640 --> 00:51:19,060
also using a corresponding macro

1656
00:51:19,060 --> 00:51:20,060
to carry it out

1657
00:51:20,060 --> 00:51:20,760
That is the restore

1658
00:51:20,760 --> 00:51:25,820
Now, between these two,

1659
00:51:25,820 --> 00:51:26,580
what we say is

1660
00:51:26,580 --> 00:51:29,900
the main core here is that

1661
00:51:29,900 --> 00:51:32,120
It's going to call our handler function.

1662
00:51:32,120 --> 00:51:33,640
Now, because the handler

1663
00:51:33,640 --> 00:51:35,880
is generally already fairly complex,

1664
00:51:35,880 --> 00:51:36,460
this part

1665
00:51:36,460 --> 00:51:38,160
is usually written in the C language.

1666
00:51:38,160 --> 00:51:41,180
So we actually call a C-language function to implement it.

1667
00:51:41,180 --> 00:51:43,580
Now, this calling process

1668
00:51:43,580 --> 00:51:45,160
is actually what we covered earlier when we talked about assembly,

1669
00:51:45,160 --> 00:51:46,160
which I introduced to you back then.

1670
00:51:46,160 --> 00:51:49,520
That is, how to call a C-language function from assembly,

1671
00:51:49,520 --> 00:51:49,840
right?

1672
00:51:49,840 --> 00:51:51,100
Now, a0, a1,

1673
00:51:51,100 --> 00:51:52,960
you still remember these, right?

1674
00:51:52,960 --> 00:51:53,600
They must be what?

1675
00:51:53,600 --> 00:51:54,620
What are they used for?

1676
00:51:54,620 --> 00:51:56,120
They're actually for passing parameters.

1677
00:51:56,120 --> 00:51:57,480
This trap handler,

1678
00:51:57,480 --> 00:51:58,940
we'll see in a moment,

1679
00:51:58,940 --> 00:52:00,140
actually takes two parameters.

1680
00:52:00,140 --> 00:52:02,020
One is used for

1681
00:52:02,020 --> 00:52:03,660
We actually just take

1682
00:52:03,660 --> 00:52:05,240
the values of mepc and mcause,

1683
00:52:05,240 --> 00:52:06,240
and pass them in.

